window.GENERATED_MODELS=[{"id":"gm-1","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Environmental Responsibility","prompt":"Some people believe that environmental problems should be solved by the government, while others think that individuals should take responsibility. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","essay":"The debate over who should shoulder the burden of environmental protection—governments or individuals—is a contentious one. While some argue that systemic change must come from policy makers, others contend that personal actions are the key. I believe that both have crucial roles, but government leadership is indispensable.\n\nOn the one hand, individuals can make a significant difference through daily choices. By reducing energy consumption, recycling, and choosing sustainable products, people can lower their carbon footprint. Moreover, grassroots movements can pressure businesses and governments to adopt greener practices. For instance, the widespread adoption of reusable bags in many countries was driven by consumer demand. Thus, individual responsibility fosters a culture of sustainability and holds leaders accountable.\n\nOn the other hand, governments possess the power to enact large-scale changes that individuals cannot. They can implement carbon taxes, invest in renewable energy infrastructure, and enforce regulations on polluting industries. Without such measures, individual efforts may be insufficient to tackle global issues like climate change. For example, the Paris Agreement relies on national commitments, not just personal actions. Therefore, government intervention is essential to create a framework within which individual efforts can be effective.\n\nIn conclusion, while personal responsibility is important, it is ultimately governments that must lead the charge against environmental degradation. Only through coordinated policies and international cooperation can we hope to mitigate the crisis. Individuals should do their part, but without strong governmental action, their impact will be limited.","annotations":["Clear thesis statement outlining both views and the writer's position.","Balanced discussion with specific examples for each side.","Strong conclusion reinforcing the need for government leadership."]},{"id":"gm-2","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Online Education","prompt":"Some people think that online education is as effective as traditional classroom learning, while others disagree. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","essay":"The rise of online education has sparked a debate about its effectiveness compared to traditional classroom learning. While some argue that online courses are equally effective, others believe that face-to-face instruction is superior. In my opinion, both modes have merits, but online education can be as effective when designed well.\n\nOn the one hand, online education offers flexibility and accessibility. Students can learn at their own pace and from any location, which is particularly beneficial for those with busy schedules or limited mobility. Moreover, online platforms often provide a wide range of resources, such as videos and interactive quizzes, that can enhance understanding. For example, many universities now offer online degrees that are highly regarded. Thus, online learning can be just as effective for motivated learners.\n\nOn the other hand, traditional classrooms provide direct interaction with teachers and peers, which can foster deeper engagement and immediate feedback. The social aspect of learning is also important for developing communication skills. For instance, group discussions and hands-on activities are difficult to replicate online. Therefore, for some subjects and learners, classroom learning may be more effective.\n\nIn conclusion, while traditional classrooms have advantages, online education can be equally effective if it incorporates interactive elements and strong support systems. The key is not the medium but the quality of instruction and the learner's motivation. As technology improves, online education will likely become even more comparable to traditional methods.","annotations":["Clear introduction presenting both sides and the writer's opinion.","Use of examples to support each viewpoint.","Concluding thought on the future of online education."]},{"id":"gm-3","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Bar Chart: Household Expenditure","prompt":"The bar chart below shows the average household expenditure in four countries in 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"The bar chart compares average household spending in four countries—Country A, Country B, Country C, and Country D—in 2020. The data is divided into five categories: housing, food, transport, leisure, and other.\n\nOverall, housing was the largest expense in all four countries, while leisure consistently received the smallest share. Country A had the highest total expenditure, whereas Country D had the lowest.\n\nIn Country A, housing accounted for 35% of household spending, followed by food at 25% and transport at 20%. Leisure and other categories made up the remaining 20%. Country B showed a similar pattern, with housing at 30%, food at 20%, and transport at 15%. However, its leisure spending was slightly higher at 10%.\n\nCountry C spent 25% on housing, 15% on food, and 10% on transport, with leisure at 5%. Country D, the lowest spender, allocated 20% to housing, 15% to food, and 10% to transport, while leisure and other each accounted for 5%. Notably, food expenditure was proportionally higher in countries with lower total spending, suggesting that basic needs take priority when budgets are tight.","annotations":["Clear overview paragraph summarising main trends.","Accurate data reporting with comparisons between countries.","Logical organisation of body paragraphs by country."]},{"id":"gm-4","type":"speaking","band":"8.0","title":"Describe a Memorable Journey","prompt":"Describe a memorable journey you have taken. You should say: where you went, how you travelled, what you did there, and explain why it was memorable.","essay":"I'd like to talk about a journey I took to Kyoto, Japan, a couple of years ago. It was a trip I'd been dreaming about for ages, and it definitely lived up to my expectations.\n\nI travelled by train from Tokyo to Kyoto using the Shinkansen, the bullet train. The journey itself was an experience—the train was incredibly fast and smooth, and I could see Mount Fuji in the distance. It was a breathtaking sight.\n\nOnce I arrived in Kyoto, I spent a week exploring the city. I visited numerous temples and shrines, including the famous Fushimi Inari Shrine with its thousands of orange torii gates. I also walked through the Arashiyama Bamboo Grove, which was serene and almost magical. The food was amazing too—I tried traditional kaiseki cuisine and street food at Nishiki Market.\n\nWhat made this journey so memorable was the blend of ancient culture and modern efficiency. Kyoto felt like a step back in time, yet everything was so well-organised. I also met some wonderful locals who were incredibly kind and helpful. The experience opened my eyes to a different way of life and left me with a deep appreciation for Japanese culture. I would love to go back someday.","annotations":["Engaging opening and clear structure covering all bullet points.","Use of descriptive language to convey the experience.","Strong conclusion explaining why it was memorable."]},{"id":"gm-5","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Work-Life Balance","prompt":"In many countries, people are working longer hours and have less free time. What are the causes of this? What solutions can you suggest?","essay":"In recent decades, there has been a noticeable trend towards longer working hours and diminished leisure time in many parts of the world. This essay will explore the reasons behind this phenomenon and propose some viable solutions.\n\nThe primary cause is economic pressure. With the cost of living rising, many individuals feel compelled to work extra hours to maintain their standard of living. Additionally, technology has blurred the boundaries between work and home life; smartphones and laptops mean employees are often expected to be available around the clock. Furthermore, in competitive job markets, workers may fear that leaving on time could be perceived as a lack of commitment, leading to a culture of presenteeism.\n\nTo address this issue, several measures could be taken. Firstly, governments could enforce stricter labour laws, such as maximum working hours and mandatory overtime pay, to protect employees. Secondly, companies should foster a culture that values output over hours spent at the desk, and managers should lead by example by not sending emails after hours. Finally, individuals can set boundaries, such as designating tech-free times, to ensure they disconnect from work.\n\nIn conclusion, while economic and technological factors drive longer working hours, a combination of legislative, corporate, and personal changes can help restore a healthier work-life balance. It is crucial for both mental well-being and overall productivity.","annotations":["Clear cause-and-effect structure with multiple reasons.","Practical solutions addressing different levels (government, company, individual).","Cohesive devices and formal vocabulary used effectively."]},{"id":"gm-6","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Line Graph: Internet Users","prompt":"The line graph below shows the percentage of the population using the internet in three countries from 2000 to 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"The line graph illustrates the proportion of people using the internet in three countries—Country X, Country Y, and Country Z—over a 20-year period from 2000 to 2020.\n\nOverall, internet usage increased significantly in all three countries, with Country X consistently having the highest percentage and Country Z the lowest. The gap between the countries narrowed over time.\n\nIn 2000, Country X had the highest internet penetration at 40%, followed by Country Y at 20% and Country Z at 5%. By 2010, Country X had risen to 70%, while Country Y reached 50% and Country Z climbed to 30%. The growth was steady in all three nations.\n\nFrom 2010 to 2020, the upward trend continued, but at a slower pace for Country X, which reached 90% by 2020. Country Y saw a more dramatic increase, reaching 85%, almost catching up with Country X. Country Z, despite starting from a low base, experienced the fastest growth rate, ending at 70% in 2020. This suggests that developing countries can rapidly adopt technology once infrastructure improves.","annotations":["Clear overview highlighting main trends and comparisons.","Accurate data selection and logical grouping of time periods.","Use of appropriate vocabulary to describe trends."]},{"id":"gm-7","type":"speaking","band":"7.5","title":"Talk about a Skill You Learned","prompt":"Talk about a skill you learned that was difficult at first but became easier with practice. You should say: what the skill was, how you learned it, why it was difficult, and how you felt when you mastered it.","essay":"I'd like to talk about learning to play the guitar, which was something I found incredibly challenging at first.\n\nI started learning about three years ago. I bought a second-hand acoustic guitar and began teaching myself using online tutorials and apps. Initially, I struggled a lot with finger pain and coordination. My fingers would hurt after just a few minutes of practice, and I couldn't switch between chords smoothly. It was frustrating because I wanted to play songs right away, but I had to start with basic exercises.\n\nThe main difficulty was building muscle memory. My fingers just wouldn't go where I wanted them to, and I often produced buzzing sounds instead of clear notes. I also had trouble keeping rhythm. There were times I felt like giving up, but I persisted by practicing a little every day.\n\nAfter about six months, things started to click. I could play simple songs, and the finger pain subsided as calluses formed. The first time I played a full song without mistakes, I felt an immense sense of accomplishment. It taught me that patience and consistent effort really pay off. Now, playing guitar is one of my favourite ways to relax.","annotations":["Clear narrative structure covering all bullet points.","Descriptive details about the challenges faced.","Reflective conclusion on the learning process."]},{"id":"gm-8","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Ageing Population","prompt":"In many countries, the proportion of older people is steadily increasing. Does this trend have more positive or negative effects on society?","essay":"The demographic shift towards an older population is a defining feature of many developed nations. While some view this as a positive development, others argue that it brings significant challenges. In my opinion, the negative effects outweigh the positive ones.\n\nOn the positive side, an ageing population can bring valuable experience and wisdom to the workforce. Older individuals often possess skills and knowledge that younger generations lack, and they can contribute to society through volunteering and mentoring. Moreover, many older people remain active and healthy, continuing to participate in the economy and community life. This can foster intergenerational solidarity and enrich social fabric.\n\nHowever, the negative consequences are more pronounced. Firstly, an increasing dependency ratio places a heavy burden on healthcare and pension systems. As the number of retirees grows, fewer working-age individuals are available to fund these services, potentially leading to higher taxes or reduced benefits. Secondly, labour shortages may occur, slowing economic growth. Thirdly, social isolation among the elderly can become a pressing issue, requiring additional social care resources.\n\nIn conclusion, while an ageing population offers some benefits, the strain on public finances and the economy is considerable. Governments must implement policies such as encouraging higher birth rates, extending working lives, and investing in automation to mitigate these effects. Without proactive measures, the negative impacts will likely dominate.","annotations":["Clear position stated in introduction and maintained throughout.","Balanced discussion with weighing of positive and negative effects.","Specific solutions proposed in conclusion."]},{"id":"gm-9","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Pie Charts: Energy Sources","prompt":"The pie charts below show the sources of energy used in a country in 2000 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"The pie charts compare the proportions of different energy sources used in a particular country in 2000 and 2020. The sources are coal, natural gas, oil, nuclear, and renewables.\n\nOverall, fossil fuels dominated both years, but there was a notable shift towards renewable energy and a decline in coal usage over the two decades.\n\nIn 2000, coal was the largest source, accounting for 40% of total energy consumption. Oil was the second largest at 30%, followed by natural gas at 20%. Nuclear and renewables made up the remaining 10%, with renewables at just 2%.\n\nBy 2020, the energy mix had changed significantly. Coal's share had dropped to 25%, while oil remained relatively stable at 28%. Natural gas increased to 25%, and nuclear rose slightly to 10%. The most striking change was in renewables, which surged to 12% of total energy use. This growth reflects the country's investment in solar and wind power. The data indicates a gradual transition away from coal towards cleaner energy sources, although fossil fuels still constitute the majority of consumption.","annotations":["Clear overview highlighting main changes over time.","Accurate data selection and comparison between years.","Use of language of change (surged, dropped, remained stable)."]},{"id":"gm-10","type":"speaking","band":"8.0","title":"Describe a Person Who Influenced You","prompt":"Describe a person who has had a significant influence on you. You should say: who the person is, how you know them, what qualities they have, and explain how they influenced you.","essay":"I'd like to talk about my grandmother, who has been a profound influence on my life. She's in her late eighties now, and we've always been very close.\n\nMy grandmother grew up in a small village and lived through difficult times, including war and poverty. Despite these hardships, she always maintained a positive outlook and a strong sense of determination. She's incredibly resilient, kind, and wise. She never had the opportunity to go to university, but she's one of the most intelligent people I know—she's self-educated and always curious about the world.\n\nShe influenced me in many ways. Firstly, she taught me the value of perseverance. Whenever I faced challenges at school or in my career, she would remind me that setbacks are temporary and that hard work pays off. Secondly, she showed me the importance of empathy. She always helped neighbours and strangers alike, expecting nothing in return. This inspired me to volunteer and pursue a career in social work. Finally, her stories about the past gave me a deep appreciation for history and the sacrifices of previous generations.\n\nIn short, my grandmother shaped my values and my character. Her strength and compassion continue to guide me every day.","annotations":["Clear structure covering all bullet points naturally.","Specific examples illustrating the person's qualities and influence.","Warm, personal tone appropriate for the topic."]},{"id":"gm-11","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"The Impact of Remote Work on Cities","prompt":"Some people believe that the rise of remote work will lead to the decline of city centres, while others think it will revitalise them. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","essay":"The pandemic-induced shift to remote work has sparked debate about the future of urban centres. While some predict a decline as offices empty, others foresee revitalisation through repurposed spaces. In my view, city centres will adapt rather than decline, provided they embrace mixed-use development.\n\nThose who anticipate decline point to reduced footfall in business districts. With fewer commuters, cafes, shops, and transport services lose revenue, potentially leading to closures and urban decay. For example, San Francisco's downtown has seen increased vacancy rates, affecting local businesses. Moreover, if people no longer need to live near work, they may move to suburbs, shrinking the tax base.\n\nConversely, optimists argue that remote work can revitalise cities by reducing congestion and pollution, making them more attractive. Empty offices can be converted into residential units, fostering vibrant communities. Cities like London are already seeing such conversions, which can support local services year-round rather than just on weekdays. Additionally, remote workers may frequent local amenities more, boosting neighbourhood economies.\n\nIn my opinion, the outcome depends on policy. Cities that invest in affordable housing, green spaces, and digital infrastructure will thrive. Those that cling to outdated office-centric models risk decline. Therefore, I believe city centres will not disappear but will transform into hubs for living, leisure, and innovation.\n\nIn conclusion, while remote work poses challenges, it also offers opportunities for urban renewal. With strategic planning, city centres can emerge more resilient and diverse than before.","annotations":["Clear position stated in introduction and maintained throughout.","Balanced discussion of both views with specific examples.","Cohesive devices used effectively (e.g., 'conversely', 'in my opinion')."]},{"id":"gm-12","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Online vs Traditional Shopping","prompt":"Some people prefer to shop online, while others prefer to visit physical stores. Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of both and give your opinion.","essay":"The debate over online versus in-store shopping has intensified with technological advancements. While online shopping offers convenience, physical stores provide sensory experiences. This essay will explore the pros and cons of each before concluding that a hybrid approach is often best.\n\nOnline shopping boasts unparalleled convenience. Consumers can browse thousands of products, compare prices, and purchase from home, saving time and travel costs. Furthermore, online platforms often offer discounts and a wider range than local shops. However, drawbacks include the inability to inspect items physically, leading to higher return rates, and potential security concerns with payment details.\n\nPhysical stores, on the other hand, allow customers to see, touch, and try products before buying, reducing the risk of dissatisfaction. They also provide immediate gratification and personal assistance from staff. Nevertheless, they may have limited stock, higher prices due to overheads, and require travel, which can be inconvenient for those with mobility issues.\n\nIn my opinion, the ideal shopping experience combines both. For items like clothing or furniture, visiting a store is preferable, while online shopping is suitable for books, electronics, and repeat purchases. Retailers should therefore integrate online and offline channels to cater to diverse needs.\n\nIn conclusion, both shopping methods have merits and demerits. Rather than replacing one with the other, consumers and businesses should leverage the strengths of each to enhance satisfaction and efficiency.","annotations":["Structured comparison with clear topic sentences for each paragraph.","Opinion is clearly stated and justified with examples.","Good range of vocabulary related to shopping and technology."]},{"id":"gm-13","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Challenges of Urbanisation","prompt":"Write about the following topic: In many countries, people are moving from rural areas to cities. What problems does this cause and what solutions can you suggest?","essay":"The global trend of urbanisation has led to mass migration from rural to urban areas, particularly in developing nations. This phenomenon creates significant challenges for cities, including housing shortages, infrastructure strain, and environmental degradation. However, with careful planning, these issues can be mitigated.\n\nOne major problem is the lack of affordable housing. As more people flock to cities, demand outstrips supply, leading to overcrowded slums and rising rents. This can result in homelessness and social unrest. Additionally, urban infrastructure such as transport, healthcare, and sanitation struggles to cope, causing congestion and disease outbreaks. Furthermore, increased population density exacerbates air and noise pollution, contributing to climate change.\n\nTo address these issues, governments must invest in affordable housing projects and expand public services. For instance, Singapore's public housing model ensures that most citizens have access to quality homes. Moreover, improving rural economies can reduce the push factors driving migration. By creating jobs and enhancing amenities in rural areas, governments can achieve a more balanced population distribution. Finally, promoting sustainable urban design, such as green buildings and efficient public transport, can minimise environmental impact.\n\nIn conclusion, while urbanisation presents serious challenges, proactive policies can transform cities into liveable spaces. A dual approach of improving urban infrastructure and rural opportunities is essential to manage this ongoing shift.","annotations":["Addresses both problems and solutions as required by the prompt.","Uses specific examples to support arguments.","Logical paragraphing with clear progression from problems to solutions."]},{"id":"gm-14","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Bar Chart: Household Energy Consumption","prompt":"The bar chart below shows the average household energy consumption in four countries (A, B, C, D) in 2020, measured in kilowatt-hours (kWh). Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"The bar chart illustrates the average energy consumption per household in four countries—A, B, C, and D—in 2020. The data is measured in kilowatt-hours (kWh). Overall, country D had the highest consumption, while country A had the lowest, with significant variations across the nations.\n\nCountry D recorded the highest usage at 10,000 kWh, which was more than double that of country C (4,500 kWh). Country B followed with 7,200 kWh, while country A consumed the least at 3,000 kWh. This indicates a wide disparity in energy use among the four countries.\n\nWhen comparing, countries B and D, which are likely more industrialised or have colder climates, show higher consumption, possibly due to greater reliance on heating or appliances. In contrast, countries A and C have lower figures, suggesting milder climates or more energy-efficient practices. The difference between the highest and lowest is 7,000 kWh, highlighting substantial inequality in household energy use.\n\nIn conclusion, the chart reveals marked differences in household energy consumption, with country D being the largest consumer and country A the smallest. These variations may reflect differences in climate, economic development, and energy policies.","annotations":["Clear overview identifying highest and lowest values.","Data is accurately reported and compared.","Appropriate language for describing trends and differences."]},{"id":"gm-15","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"The Importance of Arts Education","prompt":"Some people think that arts subjects such as music and drama are essential in schools, while others believe they are a waste of time. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","essay":"The inclusion of arts in school curricula has long been debated. Proponents argue that subjects like music and drama are vital for holistic development, whereas critics view them as non-essential compared to academic subjects. I firmly believe that arts education is indispensable for fostering creativity and emotional intelligence.\n\nAdvocates of arts education highlight its role in enhancing creativity, critical thinking, and cultural awareness. Participation in music or drama can improve cognitive abilities, boost confidence, and provide an outlet for self-expression. For instance, studies show that students engaged in arts often perform better in other subjects due to improved concentration and discipline. Moreover, arts can unite communities and preserve cultural heritage.\n\nOpponents, however, contend that arts are less important for employability and economic growth. They argue that time spent on arts could be better used for STEM subjects, which are seen as more directly linked to job opportunities. In their view, schools should prioritise subjects that lead to high-paying careers, especially in competitive economies.\n\nIn my opinion, this is a false dichotomy. Arts and sciences are complementary; many innovators credit their success to creative thinking nurtured through arts. Furthermore, a well-rounded education prepares students for diverse careers and life challenges. Therefore, arts should remain a core part of the curriculum, not an optional extra.\n\nIn conclusion, while critics raise valid concerns about resource allocation, the benefits of arts education far outweigh the drawbacks. Schools should integrate arts to develop well-rounded individuals.","annotations":["Strong thesis statement and consistent argumentation.","Counter-argument addressed and refuted effectively.","Sophisticated vocabulary and complex sentence structures."]},{"id":"gm-16","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Process Diagram: Recycling Paper","prompt":"The diagram below shows the process of recycling paper. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"The diagram illustrates the step-by-step process of recycling paper, from the collection of used paper to the production of new paper products. Overall, the process involves several stages, including collection, sorting, shredding, pulping, and rolling, and requires both manual and mechanical operations.\n\nInitially, used paper is collected from homes, offices, and recycling centres. It is then transported to a recycling plant, where it is sorted by hand to remove contaminants such as plastic and metal. After sorting, the paper is shredded into small pieces and mixed with water and chemicals to create a pulp. This pulp is then passed through a series of screens and centrifugal cleaners to remove staples and glue.\n\nNext, the cleaned pulp is subjected to a de-inking process, where air bubbles are used to remove ink. The pulp may also be bleached to whiten it. Finally, the pulp is spread onto a conveyor belt, pressed, and dried to form large rolls of recycled paper. These rolls are then cut into sheets and used to make new paper products.\n\nIn conclusion, the recycling process transforms waste paper into usable paper through multiple stages, significantly reducing the need for virgin wood pulp and conserving resources.","annotations":["Clear overview of the process stages.","Sequencing words used effectively (initially, next, finally).","Accurate description of the diagram without adding external information."]},{"id":"gm-17","type":"speaking","band":"8.0","title":"Describe a memorable journey","prompt":"Describe a memorable journey you have taken. You should say: where you went, how you travelled, what you did, and explain why it was memorable.","essay":"I'd like to talk about a memorable journey I took to Kyoto, Japan, a few years ago. I travelled there by bullet train from Tokyo, which was an experience in itself—the speed and punctuality were incredible. I spent about a week exploring the city.\n\nWhile in Kyoto, I visited numerous temples and shrines, including the famous Fushimi Inari with its thousands of orange torii gates. I also participated in a traditional tea ceremony and strolled through the Arashiyama bamboo grove. The highlight was wandering the historic Gion district, hoping to spot a geisha. The atmosphere was serene and steeped in history.\n\nWhat made this journey truly memorable was the blend of ancient traditions and modern efficiency. I was struck by how respectfully people preserved their heritage while embracing technology. Additionally, travelling alone pushed me out of my comfort zone, improving my confidence and navigation skills. The kindness of locals, who often helped me despite language barriers, left a lasting impression.\n\nOverall, the trip was a perfect mix of cultural immersion and personal growth, making it unforgettable. I often recall the peaceful gardens and the delicious kaiseki meals. It inspired me to travel more mindfully and appreciate different cultures.","annotations":["Covers all bullet points in a natural, fluent manner.","Uses descriptive language and personal reflection.","Shows range of tenses and vocabulary."]},{"id":"gm-18","type":"speaking","band":"7.5","title":"Talk about a skill you learned","prompt":"Talk about a skill you learned. You should say: what the skill is, how you learned it, how long it took, and explain how it has been useful to you.","essay":"The skill I'd like to talk about is coding, specifically Python. I learned it about three years ago when I decided to switch careers into data analysis. I started with online courses on platforms like Coursera and Codecademy, and later joined a local coding bootcamp to gain more structured practice.\n\nIt took me roughly six months of consistent study to become proficient enough to use it at work. I practised by building small projects, such as a personal budget tracker and a web scraper for news headlines. The bootcamp also provided group projects that simulated real-world scenarios.\n\nThis skill has been incredibly useful. Firstly, it enabled me to land a job as a data analyst, which I love. Secondly, it automates repetitive tasks, saving me hours each week. For example, I wrote a script to clean and visualise sales data, which impressed my manager. Beyond work, coding helps me solve everyday problems, like organising files or planning trips.\n\nMoreover, learning to code taught me resilience and problem-solving, as debugging often requires patience. It's a skill that keeps evolving, so I continue to learn. Overall, it has boosted my career and confidence, and I'd recommend it to anyone willing to invest the time.","annotations":["Provides specific details and examples for each bullet point.","Clear chronological structure with logical progression.","Reflects on personal benefits and broader applications."]},{"id":"gm-19","type":"speaking","band":"8.0","title":"Describe a person who influenced you","prompt":"Describe a person who has influenced you. You should say: who the person is, how you know them, what qualities they have, and explain how they influenced you.","essay":"The person who has had the greatest influence on me is my high school English teacher, Mrs. Sharma. I first met her when I was fifteen, and she taught me for two years. She was known for her passion for literature and her unwavering belief in her students.\n\nMrs. Sharma possessed a unique blend of patience, creativity, and empathy. She encouraged us to think critically and express our opinions, even if they differed from hers. Her classes were interactive, often involving debates and creative writing workshops. She also took a personal interest in each student's progress, offering extra help after school.\n\nHer influence on me was profound. Before her class, I was shy and hesitant to speak up. She nurtured my confidence by praising my essays and urging me to participate in public speaking competitions. I eventually won a regional debate, which was a turning point. She also instilled in me a love for reading and writing, which led me to pursue a degree in journalism.\n\nMore importantly, she taught me the value of resilience and kindness. She faced personal challenges but always remained positive and supportive. Her example inspires me to be a better person and to help others. I still keep in touch with her, and her advice continues to guide me.","annotations":["Rich descriptive vocabulary and varied sentence structures.","Clear explanation of influence with concrete examples.","Emotional engagement and reflective tone."]},{"id":"gm-20","type":"speaking","band":"7.5","title":"Talk about a place you like to relax","prompt":"Talk about a place where you like to relax. You should say: where it is, how often you go there, what you do there, and explain why it helps you relax.","essay":"A place I often go to relax is a small botanical garden near my home. It's located about a ten-minute walk away, tucked behind a quiet residential area. I try to visit at least once a week, usually on weekend mornings when it's less crowded.\n\nWhen I'm there, I like to stroll along the winding paths, admiring the variety of plants and flowers. Sometimes I sit on a bench by the pond, reading a book or simply watching the ducks. Occasionally, I bring a sketchbook and draw the scenery. The garden also has a small café where I enjoy a cup of tea.\n\nThis place helps me relax because it's a peaceful escape from the city's noise. The greenery and fresh air instantly calm my mind, and the gentle sounds of birds and water are soothing. It's a digital-free zone for me—I leave my phone in my bag and focus on the present. Moreover, the garden is rarely crowded, so I can have moments of solitude, which is refreshing.\n\nOverall, it's my sanctuary. After spending time there, I feel recharged and more positive. It's a simple pleasure that significantly improves my well-being.","annotations":["Covers all parts of the prompt with relevant details.","Uses sensory language to describe the place.","Explains relaxation benefits clearly."]},{"id":"gm-21","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Recycling and Government Responsibility","prompt":"Some people believe that recycling is the best way to reduce waste, while others argue that governments should focus on reducing the amount of packaging produced. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","essay":"The escalating volume of waste has prompted debate over whether recycling or reducing packaging is the more effective solution. While recycling is undeniably valuable, I believe that preventing waste at its source is ultimately more beneficial.\n\nOn the one hand, recycling plays a crucial role in managing the waste we already generate. It conserves raw materials, reduces landfill use, and can lower energy consumption compared to producing new goods. For example, recycling aluminium saves up to 95% of the energy required to make it from scratch. Moreover, widespread recycling programmes encourage environmental awareness among citizens, fostering a culture of sustainability.\n\nOn the other hand, recycling has limitations. Many materials are downcycled or contaminated, and a significant proportion still ends up in landfills. Furthermore, recycling relies on complex logistics and can create a false sense of security, allowing overconsumption to continue unabated. In contrast, reducing packaging tackles the problem at its root. By mandating minimal packaging or reusable alternatives, governments can prevent waste before it is created. This approach not only saves resources but also reduces the financial burden of waste management.\n\nIn my opinion, while recycling should remain part of the strategy, it is insufficient alone. Governments must prioritise reduction through legislation, such as bans on single-use plastics and incentives for eco-design. Only by combining prevention with recycling can we achieve a truly sustainable waste system.\n\nIn conclusion, although recycling offers tangible benefits, source reduction is more effective and should be the primary focus of government policy.","annotations":["Clear position stated in introduction and maintained throughout.","Balanced discussion of both views with examples and reasoning.","Cohesive devices used effectively (On the one hand, Furthermore, In contrast)."]},{"id":"gm-22","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Global Warming: Individual vs. Systemic Action","prompt":"Some argue that climate change can only be addressed by government policies, while others believe individual actions are equally important. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","essay":"The debate over whether climate change mitigation should be driven by governments or individuals is contentious. While systemic policy changes are indispensable, I contend that individual actions are also vital and can complement governmental efforts.\n\nOn the one hand, governments possess the scale and authority to enact meaningful change. Policies such as carbon pricing, renewable energy subsidies, and strict emission regulations can shift entire industries towards sustainability. For instance, the Paris Agreement relies on national commitments to reduce greenhouse gases. Without such top-down measures, individual efforts may be insufficient to meet global targets. Moreover, governments can incentivise green technologies and penalise polluters, creating a level playing field that makes sustainable choices accessible to all.\n\nOn the other hand, individual actions collectively exert significant influence. Consumer choices drive market demand; when millions opt for public transport, plant-based diets, or energy-efficient appliances, they reduce carbon footprints and signal to businesses that sustainability matters. Grassroots movements can also pressure governments to act more ambitiously. Thus, individual behaviour both directly lowers emissions and indirectly shapes policy.\n\nIn my view, the dichotomy is false: both levels are necessary. Governments must lead with robust frameworks, but without public buy-in, policies may fail. Conversely, individual efforts alone cannot achieve the required scale of change. Therefore, a synergistic approach is essential.\n\nIn conclusion, while government policies are crucial, individual actions are equally important in the fight against climate change. Only through collective effort can we hope to mitigate its effects.","annotations":["Sophisticated vocabulary and complex sentence structures.","Well-developed arguments with specific examples (Paris Agreement).","Concludes with a clear, nuanced position."]},{"id":"gm-23","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Line Graph: Electricity Consumption","prompt":"The line graph below shows the electricity consumption in terawatt-hours (TWh) of three countries (A, B, and C) from 2000 to 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"The line graph illustrates electricity consumption in three countries between 2000 and 2020. Overall, consumption increased in all three nations, with Country A consistently the highest and Country C the lowest, though the gap narrowed over time.\n\nIn 2000, Country A consumed 500 TWh, far exceeding Country B (200 TWh) and Country C (100 TWh). Over the next decade, Country A's consumption rose steadily, reaching 700 TWh in 2010, while B and C grew more gradually to 300 TWh and 150 TWh respectively.\n\nFrom 2010 to 2020, the trends diverged. Country A's growth slowed, peaking at 750 TWh in 2015 before plateauing. In contrast, Country B experienced a sharper increase, overtaking A's growth rate and reaching 600 TWh by 2020. Country C also accelerated, doubling its consumption to 300 TWh. Consequently, the gap between A and B shrank from 300 TWh in 2010 to 150 TWh in 2020.\n\nIn summary, while Country A remained the largest consumer, its dominance diminished as Countries B and C significantly increased their electricity use, particularly in the latter decade.","annotations":["Clear overview summarising main trends.","Data accurately referenced and compared.","Varied linking words and sentence structures."]},{"id":"gm-24","type":"speaking","band":"8.0","title":"Describe a Memorable Journey","prompt":"Describe a memorable journey you have taken. You should say: where you went, how you travelled, what you did there, and explain why it was memorable.","essay":"I’d like to talk about a trip I took to Kyoto, Japan, a few years ago. It was a journey that left a deep impression on me, not just because of the stunning sights but also due to the cultural immersion.\n\nI travelled there by train, specifically the Shinkansen bullet train from Tokyo. The ride itself was an experience—smooth, incredibly punctual, and offering fleeting glimpses of Mount Fuji along the way. Upon arriving, I spent a week exploring ancient temples, strolling through bamboo groves, and participating in a traditional tea ceremony. I also visited the Fushimi Inari Shrine, with its thousands of vermilion torii gates, which was absolutely breathtaking.\n\nWhat made this journey truly memorable was the contrast between the serene, timeless atmosphere of Kyoto and the bustling modernity of Tokyo. It felt like stepping into a different era. Moreover, the people I met were incredibly kind and helpful, even when language barriers arose. I remember getting lost in Gion and a local shopkeeper patiently drawing me a map. That small act of kindness made me feel welcomed.\n\nOverall, the trip was a perfect blend of adventure, tranquillity, and human connection. It broadened my perspective and sparked a lasting interest in Japanese culture. I often look back on it as one of the most enriching experiences of my life.","annotations":["Rich descriptive vocabulary and idiomatic expressions.","Engaging narrative with personal anecdotes.","Clear structure covering all bullet points."]},{"id":"gm-25","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Pie Charts: Household Expenditure","prompt":"The pie charts below show the average household expenditure in a country in 1990 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"The pie charts compare the average household spending patterns in a particular country in 1990 and 2020. Overall, while food and housing remained the largest categories, there were notable shifts: expenditure on food decreased, whereas spending on transport and leisure increased.\n\nIn 1990, food accounted for 35% of household budgets, the highest proportion, followed by housing at 25%. Transport and leisure each represented 15%, while clothing and other items made up the remaining 10%. By 2020, the distribution had changed considerably. Food's share fell to 25%, and housing slightly decreased to 22%. In contrast, transport rose to 20%, and leisure grew to 18%. Clothing dropped to 8%, and other categories remained at 7%.\n\nThe most striking change was the 10% decline in food expenditure, likely reflecting improved living standards. Meanwhile, the combined increase in transport and leisure (from 30% to 38%) suggests a greater emphasis on mobility and recreation. Housing's modest drop may indicate relatively stable costs or smaller households.\n\nIn summary, although food and housing continued to dominate, the period saw a clear reallocation of household budgets towards transport and leisure activities.","annotations":["Precise data reporting and comparison.","Coherent paragraphing with clear topic sentences.","Effective use of vocabulary for trends (decline, reallocation)."]},{"id":"gm-26","type":"speaking","band":"7.5","title":"Describe a Skill You Learned","prompt":"Describe a skill you learned that was difficult at first. You should say: what the skill was, how you learned it, why it was difficult, and how you felt when you mastered it.","essay":"I’d like to talk about learning to play the guitar, which was quite challenging initially. I started about five years ago when I was in high school. My motivation came from wanting to accompany myself while singing, and I admired musicians who could play effortlessly.\n\nI learned mainly through online tutorials and a lot of practice. At first, I struggled with finger pain and coordination. My fingers would get sore, and I couldn’t switch chords smoothly. It was frustrating because I expected quick progress, but it took weeks just to play a simple song. I also had trouble with strumming patterns and keeping rhythm.\n\nWhat made it difficult was the physical demand and the need for muscle memory. Unlike theoretical subjects, guitar requires repetitive practice to build dexterity. There were times I wanted to give up, but I persisted by setting small goals, like learning one new chord each week.\n\nEventually, after a few months, I could play a few songs from start to finish. The feeling of mastery was incredibly rewarding—I felt a sense of accomplishment and confidence. It taught me the value of patience and consistent effort. Now, playing guitar is a source of relaxation and joy, and I’m glad I didn’t quit.","annotations":["Personal and reflective tone appropriate for speaking.","Clear chronological structure and covering all points.","Good use of descriptive phrases (muscle memory, dexterity)."]},{"id":"gm-27","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"University Education and Job Preparation","prompt":"Some people think that universities should provide graduates with the knowledge and skills needed in the workplace. Others think that the true function of a university should be to give access to knowledge for its own sake. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","essay":"The role of universities has long been debated: should they focus on vocational training or on the pursuit of knowledge for its own sake? While both perspectives have merit, I believe that universities should balance both aims, though their primary purpose is to cultivate critical thinking and broad intellectual growth.\n\nOn the one hand, those who advocate for job-oriented education argue that universities should equip graduates with practical skills to enhance employability. In today's competitive job market, employers seek candidates with specific competencies, such as coding, data analysis, or project management. If universities neglect these, graduates may struggle to find work, leading to high unemployment and wasted potential. Moreover, vocational courses can boost economic productivity by supplying skilled workers.\n\nOn the other hand, the traditional view holds that universities are not merely training grounds but institutions for intellectual exploration. Subjects like philosophy, literature, and history may not have direct vocational links, but they foster critical thinking, creativity, and cultural awareness. These qualities are invaluable for personal development and for a democratic society. Furthermore, a broad education can adapt to changing job markets, whereas narrow training may become obsolete.\n\nIn my opinion, the ideal university education integrates both. While practical skills are important, they should be built on a foundation of broad knowledge. Universities should offer internships and career services alongside a rigorous academic curriculum.\n\nIn conclusion, although employability is crucial, the true function of a university is to nurture well-rounded individuals. A balanced approach serves both students and society best.","annotations":["Clear thesis and balanced discussion.","Relevant examples and reasoning for each view.","Cohesive paragraphing with signposting language."]},{"id":"gm-28","type":"speaking","band":"8.0","title":"Describe a Person Who Influenced You","prompt":"Describe a person who has had a significant influence on you. You should say: who this person is, how you know them, what qualities they have, and explain how they influenced you.","essay":"I’d like to talk about my grandmother, who has been a profound influence on my life. She’s in her late seventies now, and I’ve known her my entire life, of course. She grew up in a small village and later moved to the city, and her life story is one of resilience and kindness.\n\nShe possesses a remarkable combination of wisdom, patience, and generosity. Despite facing many hardships, she always maintains a positive outlook and never complains. She’s also an incredible listener—when I was growing up, she would always make time to hear my worries and offer gentle advice without being judgmental.\n\nHer influence on me has been multifaceted. Firstly, she taught me the value of perseverance. When I struggled with exams or personal setbacks, she would remind me that challenges are temporary and that effort matters more than immediate results. Secondly, she modelled compassion. She volunteers at a local community centre and helps neighbours in need, which inspired me to get involved in volunteering myself. Thirdly, she encouraged my curiosity, often telling me stories about history and nature, which sparked my love for learning.\n\nIn essence, my grandmother shaped my character and values. Her quiet strength and unwavering support have made me the person I am today, and I aspire to live up to her example.","annotations":["Deep personal reflection with specific examples.","Varied and precise vocabulary (multifaceted, perseverance).","Fluent and coherent narrative structure."]},{"id":"gm-29","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Prison vs. Rehabilitation","prompt":"Some people think that the best way to reduce crime is to give longer prison sentences. Others, however, believe there are better alternative ways of reducing crime. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","essay":"The effectiveness of long prison sentences as a crime deterrent is a contentious issue. While some argue that extended incarceration reduces crime, others advocate for alternative approaches such as rehabilitation and education. I believe that while prison sentences have a role, rehabilitation is ultimately more effective in reducing recidivism and building safer communities.\n\nOn the one hand, proponents of longer sentences argue that they incapacitate offenders and deter potential criminals. By keeping dangerous individuals off the streets for extended periods, society is protected. Moreover, the threat of lengthy imprisonment may discourage people from committing crimes, especially those considering serious offences. For instance, harsh penalties for drug trafficking in some countries have been associated with lower crime rates.\n\nOn the other hand, critics point out that long sentences often fail to address the root causes of crime, such as poverty, addiction, or lack of education. Prisons can become breeding grounds for further criminality, and released inmates may struggle to reintegrate, leading to high reoffending rates. In contrast, rehabilitation programmes—vocational training, therapy, and education—equip offenders with skills to lead law-abiding lives. Countries like Norway, which focus on rehabilitation, have remarkably low recidivism rates.\n\nIn my opinion, a balanced approach is needed. Serious and violent crimes warrant imprisonment, but for many non-violent offences, community-based rehabilitation is more humane and effective. Investing in education and mental health support can prevent crime before it occurs.\n\nIn conclusion, while longer sentences may offer short-term protection, they are not the best solution. Rehabilitation and social investment are more sustainable ways to reduce crime.","annotations":["Strong critical analysis with global examples (Norway).","Sophisticated cohesion and paragraph transitions.","Nuanced opinion that acknowledges both sides."]},{"id":"gm-30","type":"speaking","band":"7.5","title":"Describe a Place You Like to Relax","prompt":"Describe a place you like to go to relax. You should say: where it is, how often you go there, what you do there, and explain why it helps you relax.","essay":"I’d like to talk about my favourite place to relax, which is a small park near my home. It’s a tranquil spot with a pond, some old trees, and plenty of benches. I usually go there two or three times a week, especially after work or on weekend mornings.\n\nWhen I’m there, I like to sit on a bench by the pond and read a book or simply watch the ducks. Sometimes I take a leisurely walk along the winding paths, or I just close my eyes and listen to the birds. I often bring a flask of tea and enjoy a quiet moment away from screens and noise.\n\nWhat makes this place so relaxing is its peaceful atmosphere. The park is rarely crowded, and the natural surroundings—the greenery, the water, the gentle breeze—have a calming effect on me. It’s a stark contrast to the hustle and bustle of daily life. Being in nature helps me clear my mind, reduce stress, and gain perspective. I find that even 20 minutes there can rejuvenate me.\n\nMoreover, it’s conveniently close, so I don’t need to plan a big trip. It’s my little sanctuary. I always leave feeling refreshed and more positive. That’s why I treasure this place so much.","annotations":["Sensory details create a vivid picture.","Natural spoken language with appropriate fillers.","Clear explanation of why it aids relaxation."]},{"id":"gm-31","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Writing Task 2 - Banning Private Cars in City Centres","prompt":"Some cities have banned private cars from their centres to reduce congestion and pollution. Do the advantages of this policy outweigh the disadvantages?","essay":"In recent years, a number of urban authorities have prohibited private vehicles from central districts in an attempt to tackle traffic congestion and air pollution. While this approach has some drawbacks, I believe that the benefits are more significant.\n\nThe most obvious disadvantage is the inconvenience caused to certain groups. For instance, people with mobility difficulties, the elderly, and those who need to transport heavy goods may find it difficult to switch to public transport or cycling. Moreover, small businesses in pedestrianised zones might suffer if customers who rely on cars choose to shop elsewhere. In the short term, such a policy could therefore lead to economic losses and public resentment.\n\nHowever, the advantages are considerable. Firstly, removing cars from city centres dramatically reduces air pollution, which is linked to respiratory diseases. Cleaner air improves public health and lowers healthcare costs. Secondly, pedestrianised areas often become more attractive, boosting tourism and local commerce as people are more willing to stroll and shop. Thirdly, the reduction in noise and accidents creates a safer, more pleasant environment. Cities such as Copenhagen and Amsterdam have demonstrated that restricting cars can lead to vibrant, economically successful centres.\n\nIn conclusion, although banning private cars may inconvenience some individuals and businesses, the environmental, health, and economic benefits for the wider community are far greater. With proper support for alternative transport, the disadvantages can be minimised.","annotations":["Clear position stated in the introduction and maintained throughout.","Balanced discussion of disadvantages and advantages before a reasoned conclusion.","Varied cohesive devices and topic-specific vocabulary used effectively."]},{"id":"gm-32","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Writing Task 2 - University Education Should Be Free","prompt":"Some people believe that university education should be free for all students, regardless of their background. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","essay":"The proposition that tertiary education should be funded entirely by the state, thereby eliminating tuition fees for all students, is a contentious one. While I acknowledge the potential benefits of such a policy, I believe that a completely free system is neither feasible nor entirely desirable.\n\nOn the one hand, abolishing fees would undoubtedly promote social mobility. Talented individuals from low-income families, who might otherwise be deterred by the prospect of debt, would have the opportunity to pursue higher education. This could lead to a more educated workforce and reduce inequality. Furthermore, a well-educated population tends to drive economic growth and innovation, benefiting society as a whole.\n\nHowever, making university free for everyone, including those who can afford to pay, would place an enormous burden on public finances. Governments would need to either raise taxes significantly or divert funding from other essential services such as healthcare and primary education. Additionally, free education might encourage some students to enrol without serious commitment, leading to high dropout rates and wasted resources. A more targeted approach, such as means-tested grants or income-contingent loans, would ensure that support reaches those who genuinely need it while maintaining fiscal sustainability.\n\nIn conclusion, while I agree that financial barriers should not prevent capable students from attending university, I do not believe that free education for all is the most effective solution. A system of targeted financial aid is preferable, as it balances equity with economic practicality.","annotations":["Sophisticated introduction with a clear thesis and nuanced position.","Well-developed arguments with logical progression and counter-argument.","Precise academic vocabulary and complex grammatical structures used accurately."]},{"id":"gm-33","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Writing Task 1 - Bar Chart of Household Energy Consumption","prompt":"The bar chart below shows the average monthly household energy consumption in three countries (A, B, and C) in 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"The bar chart compares the average monthly household energy consumption in three countries, labelled A, B, and C, in the year 2020. The data is measured in kilowatt-hours (kWh).\n\nOverall, Country A had the highest energy consumption across all months, while Country C consistently used the least. In all three countries, consumption peaked in the winter months and dropped to its lowest point in summer.\n\nIn Country A, energy use was highest in January, at approximately 1,200 kWh, and fell to around 600 kWh in July. Country B followed a similar pattern but at lower levels, with a maximum of about 900 kWh in January and a minimum of 400 kWh in July. Country C, by contrast, showed the least variation, ranging from 500 kWh in January to 300 kWh in July.\n\nIt is also notable that the gap between the countries was most pronounced in winter. In January, Country A consumed twice as much as Country C, whereas by July the difference had narrowed considerably. This suggests that heating needs, rather than baseline electricity use, account for the majority of the disparity.","annotations":["Clear overview highlighting the main trends and differences.","Accurate data selection and comparisons between countries.","Varied sentence structures and appropriate reporting language."]},{"id":"gm-34","type":"speaking","band":"7.5","title":"Speaking Part 2 - Describe a Skill You Learned","prompt":"Describe a skill you learned that you find useful. You should say: what the skill is, when and how you learned it, how you use it, and explain why it is useful to you.","essay":"I'd like to talk about learning to cook, which I picked up about five years ago when I moved out of my parents' home. At first, I could barely boil an egg, but I gradually taught myself by watching online tutorials and following simple recipes. It was a slow process, and I made plenty of mistakes along the way, but I persisted because I was determined to eat healthily and save money.\n\nNow, I use this skill every day. I plan my meals for the week, do a single grocery shop, and prepare most of my own food from scratch. I've become quite confident, and I even enjoy experimenting with new dishes from different cuisines, like Thai curries or Italian pasta.\n\nThe reason this skill is so useful to me is that it has improved my life in several ways. Firstly, it's much cheaper than eating out or ordering takeaway, which has helped me save a significant amount. Secondly, I have much more control over what I eat, so I feel healthier and more energetic. Finally, cooking has become a creative outlet and a way to relax after a long day. I've also found that it's a great way to connect with friends when I invite them over for dinner. So, overall, it's a skill I'd recommend to everyone.","annotations":["Covers all bullet points in a natural, extended manner.","Uses a range of tenses and connectives appropriately.","Shows enthusiasm and personal reflection with idiomatic language."]},{"id":"gm-35","type":"speaking","band":"8.0","title":"Speaking Part 3 - The Impact of Technology on Communication","prompt":"In what ways has technology changed the way people communicate? Do you think these changes are positive or negative?","essay":"Technology has revolutionised communication in ways we couldn't have imagined a few decades ago. The most obvious change is the speed and convenience it offers. We can now send a message halfway around the world in seconds, and video calls allow us to see and speak to loved ones as if they were in the same room. This has made maintaining long-distance relationships far easier and has facilitated global collaboration in business and education.\n\nHowever, I believe these changes are a double-edged sword. On the positive side, technology has democratised communication, giving a voice to people who might otherwise be marginalised. Social media, for instance, has enabled activists to organise and spread awareness rapidly. On the negative side, there are concerns about the quality of our interactions. People often spend more time on their phones than engaging with those physically present, leading to a sense of isolation. Moreover, the anonymity of online platforms can encourage rudeness and misinformation.\n\nOn balance, I think the benefits outweigh the drawbacks, but we need to be mindful. It's crucial to strike a balance between digital and face-to-face communication. If we use technology wisely, it can enhance rather than replace genuine human connection.","annotations":["Addresses both parts of the question with a clear stance.","Develops arguments with specific examples and contrasts.","Uses advanced vocabulary and complex sentences naturally."]},{"id":"gm-36","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Writing Task 2 - Should Governments Spend Money on Space Exploration?","prompt":"Some people think that governments should spend money on space exploration, while others believe it is a waste of money that could be better spent on public services. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","essay":"The allocation of public funds to space exploration is a topic of considerable debate. While some argue that it is an essential investment in humanity's future, others contend that the money would be better used to address pressing social issues. This essay will examine both perspectives before offering my own view.\n\nProponents of space exploration highlight its potential to drive innovation and inspire future generations. Many technologies we take for granted, such as satellite communications and medical imaging, originated from space research. Furthermore, exploring the cosmos could lead to the discovery of resources or even habitable planets, which might be crucial for the long-term survival of our species. The pursuit of knowledge and the spirit of discovery are also seen as intrinsically valuable.\n\nOpponents, however, argue that with so many people lacking access to basic healthcare, education, and clean water, spending billions on space missions is morally questionable. They believe that governments have a duty to prioritise the immediate needs of their citizens. Moreover, the benefits of space exploration are often uncertain and may not materialise for decades, whereas investment in public services yields tangible and immediate results.\n\nIn my opinion, while public services must remain a priority, space exploration should not be neglected entirely. A balanced approach, where a modest proportion of the budget is dedicated to scientific research, is advisable. This ensures we continue to advance as a civilisation while also addressing urgent social challenges.","annotations":["Covers both views fairly and clearly states the writer's opinion.","Uses cohesive paragraphs and a formal, academic tone.","Provides relevant examples and a balanced conclusion."]},{"id":"gm-37","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Writing Task 1 - Line Graph of Internet Users","prompt":"The line graph below shows the percentage of the population using the internet in three different countries between 2000 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"The line graph illustrates the proportion of people using the internet in three countries over a 20-year period from 2000 to 2020.\n\nOverall, internet usage increased significantly in all three countries. Country X had the highest percentage throughout the period, while Country Z had the lowest. The gap between the countries narrowed considerably by 2020.\n\nIn 2000, only about 10% of the population in Country X used the internet, compared to 5% in Country Y and just 2% in Country Z. Over the next decade, usage in Country X rose steadily, reaching 50% by 2010. Country Y experienced a more dramatic increase, from 5% to 40% in the same period. Country Z, however, grew more slowly, reaching only 15% by 2010.\n\nFrom 2010 onwards, the growth in all three countries accelerated. By 2020, Country X had reached 90%, Country Y 80%, and Country Z 60%. The most striking feature is the rapid convergence of the three countries, with Country Z more than doubling its usage in the final five years, suggesting significant improvements in infrastructure and access.","annotations":["Clear overview of trends and key comparisons.","Accurate use of data and time phrases.","Varied vocabulary for describing trends and changes."]},{"id":"gm-38","type":"speaking","band":"7.5","title":"Speaking Part 1 - Work and Studies","prompt":"Let's talk about your work or studies. What do you do? Do you enjoy it? Why or why not?","essay":"I'm currently a final-year student at university, majoring in environmental science. So, my main focus is my studies, but I also have a part-time job at a local café to help cover my expenses.\n\nDo I enjoy my studies? Yes, absolutely. I've always been passionate about the environment, and my course allows me to explore topics like climate change, conservation, and sustainable development. The best part is the fieldwork—we often go on trips to collect data, which makes the learning very hands-on and practical. It can be demanding, especially with deadlines, but I find it incredibly rewarding.\n\nAs for my café job, it's quite different. It's busy and sometimes stressful, but I enjoy interacting with customers and my colleagues. It's also taught me valuable skills like time management and teamwork, which will be useful in any career. Of course, balancing work and study can be tough, but I've learned to manage my time effectively. Overall, I feel lucky to be doing something I'm passionate about while gaining work experience.","annotations":["Directly answers the questions with relevant details.","Uses a mix of simple and complex sentences naturally.","Shows enthusiasm and provides specific examples."]},{"id":"gm-39","type":"speaking","band":"8.0","title":"Speaking Part 2 - Describe a Memorable Journey","prompt":"Describe a memorable journey you have taken. You should say: where you went, how you travelled, what you did there, and explain why it was memorable.","essay":"I'd like to talk about a trip I took to Japan a couple of years ago. It was a two-week adventure, and I travelled with my best friend. We flew from London to Tokyo, and then used the famous bullet trains to travel between cities like Kyoto, Osaka, and Hiroshima. The trains were incredibly punctual and comfortable, which made getting around very easy.\n\nWhile we were there, we immersed ourselves in the culture. We visited ancient temples, wandered through bamboo forests, and even participated in a traditional tea ceremony. We also ate our weight in sushi and ramen! One of the highlights was staying in a ryokan, a traditional Japanese inn, where we slept on tatami mats and soaked in an onsen, a hot spring bath.\n\nThe reason this journey was so memorable is that it was a complete sensory overload in the best possible way. The contrast between the ultra-modern cities and the serene, ancient traditions was fascinating. It also pushed me out of my comfort zone, as I had to navigate a country where I didn't speak the language. The kindness and politeness of the people we met left a lasting impression on me. It was truly a trip of a lifetime, and I'd go back in a heartbeat.","annotations":["Rich descriptive detail and varied vocabulary.","Well-structured narrative covering all bullet points.","Expresses personal feelings and reflection effectively."]},{"id":"gm-40","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Writing Task 2 - The Importance of Arts in Education","prompt":"Some people believe that arts subjects such as music and drama are as important as academic subjects in a child's education. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","essay":"The role of arts in the school curriculum is a subject of ongoing debate. While some argue that academic subjects should take precedence, others believe that arts are equally vital. I strongly agree that arts education is just as important as traditional academic subjects.\n\nFirstly, the arts foster creativity and critical thinking, which are essential skills in the modern world. Studying music, for example, enhances mathematical and spatial reasoning, while drama builds confidence and communication skills. These are not merely hobbies; they are fundamental to a well-rounded education. Moreover, arts subjects provide an outlet for self-expression and emotional development, which is crucial for children's mental well-being.\n\nSecondly, the arts can improve academic performance in other areas. Research has shown that students who participate in arts programmes often achieve better results in subjects like maths and science. This is because the arts teach discipline, perseverance, and the ability to think outside the box. Furthermore, for some children, the arts may be the only subject where they excel, giving them a sense of achievement and motivation to stay in school.\n\nIn conclusion, I firmly believe that arts should be an integral part of the curriculum, alongside academic subjects. A balanced education that includes both is essential for developing well-rounded, creative, and emotionally intelligent individuals.","annotations":["Clear agreement and consistent argument throughout.","Uses examples and research to support points.","Cohesive devices and formal vocabulary used appropriately."]},{"id":"gm-41","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Governments should spend money on public services rather than arts","prompt":"Some people believe that governments should spend money on public services rather than on the arts. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","essay":"It is often argued that government funding should be directed towards essential public services rather than the arts. While I agree that core services like healthcare and education must take priority, I believe that allocating some resources to the arts is also important for a balanced society.\n\nOn the one hand, public services are fundamental to meeting basic human needs. Without adequate funding, hospitals cannot provide timely treatment, schools cannot deliver quality education, and infrastructure such as roads and public transport may deteriorate. These services directly affect the well-being of all citizens, particularly the most vulnerable. Therefore, it is reasonable for governments to ensure that these sectors receive the bulk of their budgets.\n\nOn the other hand, the arts contribute significantly to cultural identity and social cohesion. Museums, theatres, and community art programmes offer opportunities for creativity and reflection, which enhance quality of life. They also generate employment and attract tourism, providing economic benefits. Moreover, exposure to the arts can support education by developing critical thinking and empathy in students. Neglecting the arts entirely could lead to a society that is materially secure but culturally impoverished.\n\nIn conclusion, while public services must remain the top priority, I disagree that arts funding should be eliminated. A sensible approach is to maintain essential services while providing modest support to the arts, ensuring both practical needs and cultural enrichment are met. This balanced allocation will ultimately benefit society as a whole.","annotations":["Clear position stated in introduction and maintained throughout","Balanced discussion with examples for both sides","Cohesive devices used effectively (on the one hand, moreover, in conclusion)"]},{"id":"gm-42","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"The chart below shows the percentage of households with internet access in three countries","prompt":"The chart below shows the percentage of households with internet access in three countries between 2000 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"The line graph illustrates the proportion of households with internet access in three countries—Country A, Country B, and Country C—over a 20-year period from 2000 to 2020.\n\nOverall, internet access increased significantly in all three countries, with Country A consistently having the highest percentage and Country C the lowest. However, the gap between them narrowed over time, as Country C experienced the most rapid growth.\n\nIn 2000, only 10% of households in Country A had internet access, while the figures for Country B and Country C were 5% and 2% respectively. By 2010, access in Country A had risen to 60%, and Country B to 40%, whereas Country C remained relatively low at 15%. Over the next decade, Country A's growth slowed, reaching 90% in 2020. Country B followed a similar but slightly lower trajectory, ending at 80%.\n\nIn contrast, Country C saw a dramatic surge after 2010, with its percentage climbing from 15% to 70% by 2020. This sharp increase meant that the disparity between Country C and the other two countries was considerably reduced. Indeed, while Country A and Country B had near-universal access by 2020, Country C's rapid expansion highlighted the potential for late adopters to catch up quickly.","annotations":["Clear overview summarising main trends","Data points accurately selected and compared","Varied sentence structures and precise vocabulary (trajectory, surge, disparity)"]},{"id":"gm-43","type":"speaking","band":"7.5","title":"Describe a skill you learned that was difficult at first","prompt":"Describe a skill you learned that was difficult at first. You should say: what the skill was, how you learned it, why it was difficult, and explain how you felt when you finally mastered it.","essay":"I'd like to talk about learning to play the guitar. I started learning about five years ago, mainly because I wanted to be able to play songs for my friends and family. I took some online lessons and also practiced with a friend who had been playing for years.\n\nAt first, it was incredibly difficult. My fingers hurt from pressing the strings, and I couldn't switch between chords smoothly. I remember feeling frustrated because even simple songs seemed impossible. I almost gave up several times, but my friend encouraged me to keep going. I started practicing just 15 minutes a day, focusing on one chord at a time. Gradually, my fingers became stronger and my coordination improved.\n\nAfter about six months, I could play a few basic songs. The turning point was when I managed to play a whole song without stopping. I felt a huge sense of achievement. It wasn't perfect, but it was recognizable. That moment motivated me to continue and learn more complex pieces.\n\nNow, I can play quite a few songs, and I really enjoy it. Looking back, I realize that patience and consistent practice are key. The feeling of mastering something that once seemed impossible is incredibly rewarding. It taught me that with perseverance, I can overcome challenges.","annotations":["Natural spoken style with contractions and fillers","Clear structure following the bullet points","Varied vocabulary and range of tenses"]},{"id":"gm-44","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Many people prefer to work from home rather than in an office","prompt":"Many people now prefer to work from home rather than in a traditional office. What are the advantages and disadvantages of this trend?","essay":"In recent years, working from home has become increasingly common, largely due to advances in technology and changes in workplace culture. While this trend offers several benefits, it also presents some drawbacks.\n\nOne major advantage is flexibility. Employees can often choose their own working hours, which helps them balance professional and personal responsibilities. For example, parents can pick up their children from school or attend appointments without taking leave. Additionally, remote work eliminates commuting time and costs, reducing stress and environmental pollution. Companies also benefit by saving on office space and being able to hire talent from anywhere in the world.\n\nHowever, there are notable disadvantages. One common issue is isolation. Without face-to-face interaction, employees may feel lonely and disconnected from their colleagues. This can lead to reduced teamwork and a lack of creative collaboration. Another drawback is the blurring of boundaries between work and home life. When the office is in the living room, it can be difficult to switch off, leading to longer hours and potential burnout. Furthermore, some people struggle with distractions at home, such as household chores or family members, which can lower productivity.\n\nIn conclusion, while working from home offers flexibility and cost savings, it also poses challenges such as social isolation and difficulty maintaining work-life balance. The ideal solution may be a hybrid model, combining the benefits of both remote and office work.","annotations":["Clear advantages and disadvantages with specific examples","Logical paragraphing and cohesive devices","Conclusion offers a balanced solution"]},{"id":"gm-45","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Some people think that the best way to reduce crime is to give longer prison sentences","prompt":"Some people think that the best way to reduce crime is to give longer prison sentences. Others, however, believe there are better alternative ways of reducing crime. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","essay":"The question of how to effectively reduce crime has sparked considerable debate. While some argue that longer prison sentences are the most effective deterrent, others contend that alternative measures, such as rehabilitation and social intervention, yield better results. This essay will examine both perspectives before offering my own view.\n\nProponents of longer sentences believe that the threat of extended imprisonment discourages potential offenders. They argue that if criminals know they will face severe punishment, they are less likely to commit crimes. Moreover, keeping repeat offenders behind bars for longer periods protects society by preventing them from reoffending. For instance, in some jurisdictions, mandatory minimum sentences have been introduced for serious crimes, and supporters claim this has contributed to lower crime rates.\n\nHowever, critics point out that longer sentences do little to address the root causes of crime, such as poverty, lack of education, and addiction. They advocate for alternatives like rehabilitation programmes, community service, and educational opportunities within prisons. These approaches aim to reform offenders and reintegrate them into society, reducing recidivism. For example, in Norway, prisons focus on rehabilitation, and the country has one of the lowest reoffending rates globally. Additionally, preventive measures like youth programmes and mental health support can stop crime before it occurs.\n\nIn my opinion, while punishment is necessary, relying solely on longer sentences is ineffective. A balanced approach that combines appropriate punishment with rehabilitation and social support is more likely to reduce crime sustainably. Therefore, governments should invest in both secure prisons and community-based programmes.","annotations":["Both views discussed in separate paragraphs","Opinion clearly stated in conclusion","Effective use of examples and counter-arguments"]},{"id":"gm-46","type":"speaking","band":"8.0","title":"Describe a memorable journey you have taken","prompt":"Describe a memorable journey you have taken. You should say: where you went, how you travelled, why it was memorable, and explain how you felt about it.","essay":"I'd like to talk about a trip I took to Japan a couple of years ago. It was a two-week journey, and I travelled by plane, train, and even a bullet train. I went with my sister, and we visited Tokyo, Kyoto, and Osaka.\n\nWhat made it so memorable was the blend of traditional and modern culture. In Kyoto, we visited ancient temples and participated in a tea ceremony, which was incredibly serene. In Tokyo, we experienced the bustling Shibuya crossing and the futuristic technology. The contrast was fascinating. Also, the people were incredibly polite and helpful, even though we didn't speak much Japanese.\n\nOne particular moment stands out: we got lost in a small town near Mount Fuji, and a local family invited us into their home for dinner. They didn't speak English, but we communicated through gestures and a translation app. That act of kindness touched me deeply.\n\nOverall, the journey was eye-opening. It broadened my perspective on how different cultures can coexist with such harmony. I felt a mix of excitement, awe, and gratitude. Since then, I've been more eager to travel and learn about other ways of life. It was truly a once-in-a-lifetime experience.","annotations":["Rich descriptive language and personal anecdotes","Fluent delivery with natural pauses and intonation","Strong emotional engagement and reflection"]},{"id":"gm-47","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"The table below shows the average monthly expenditure of a family in four countries","prompt":"The table below shows the average monthly expenditure of a family in four countries (A, B, C, and D) on food, housing, education, and leisure in 2023. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"The table compares the average monthly spending of families in four countries—A, B, C, and D—across four categories: food, housing, education, and leisure in 2023.\n\nOverall, housing was the largest expense in all four countries, while leisure received the smallest proportion of the budget. Country A had the highest total expenditure, whereas Country D had the lowest.\n\nIn Country A, families spent the most on housing at $1,200, followed by food at $800, education at $500, and leisure at $300. Country B showed a similar pattern but with lower figures: housing $900, food $600, education $400, and leisure $200. Country C spent $700 on housing, $500 on food, $300 on education, and $150 on leisure. Country D, with the lowest overall spending, allocated $500 to housing, $400 to food, $200 to education, and $100 to leisure.\n\nIt is notable that the ratio of housing to total expenditure was highest in Country A (about 43%), while in Country D it was around 42%. Education and leisure consistently accounted for smaller shares. The data suggests that families in all four countries prioritise basic needs over discretionary spending.","annotations":["Clear overview of main trends and exceptions","Precise data selection and logical grouping","Varied comparisons and appropriate linking words"]},{"id":"gm-48","type":"speaking","band":"7.5","title":"Describe a person who has influenced you","prompt":"Describe a person who has influenced you. You should say: who the person is, how you know them, what qualities they have, and explain how they have influenced you.","essay":"I'd like to talk about my grandmother, who has had a profound influence on my life. She's in her late seventies now, and I've known her all my life. She lives in a small village, and I used to spend my summer holidays with her when I was a child.\n\nShe has many admirable qualities. She's incredibly resilient and optimistic. Despite facing many hardships, she always maintains a positive attitude. She's also very generous and kind. She would often help neighbours without expecting anything in return. Another quality is her wisdom. She has a knack for giving practical advice that always seems to work.\n\nHer influence on me has been significant. She taught me the value of hard work and perseverance. When I was struggling with my studies, she would tell me stories of how she overcame obstacles, which motivated me to keep going. She also instilled in me a sense of compassion. Seeing her help others made me want to do the same. Even now, whenever I face a difficult decision, I think about what she would do.\n\nOverall, she has shaped my character and values. I'm grateful for her guidance and hope to pass on her wisdom to others.","annotations":["Clear organisation and natural flow","Specific examples of influence","Range of vocabulary and grammatical structures"]},{"id":"gm-49","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"International tourism has become a major industry","prompt":"International tourism has become a major industry. Some people think it creates tension rather than understanding between cultures. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","essay":"The growth of international tourism has sparked debate about whether it fosters cross-cultural understanding or generates tension. While I acknowledge that tourism can sometimes lead to friction, I largely disagree that it creates more tension than understanding. In fact, it often promotes mutual appreciation and economic benefits.\n\nOn the one hand, tourism can cause tension when it leads to cultural insensitivity or overcrowding. For example, tourists who disrespect local customs or behave rudely can offend host communities. Additionally, mass tourism may strain infrastructure and lead to environmental degradation, causing resentment among locals. In some cases, the commodification of culture can trivialise traditions, which may be seen as disrespectful.\n\nOn the other hand, tourism generally fosters understanding by exposing people to different ways of life. When travellers interact respectfully with locals, they gain insights into foreign cultures, breaking down stereotypes. For instance, homestays and cultural tours allow tourists to engage authentically, promoting empathy. Moreover, tourism creates jobs and income, which can improve living standards and fund cultural preservation. Many destinations invest in maintaining heritage sites precisely because tourists value them.\n\nIn my opinion, the tensions that arise are often due to poor management rather than tourism itself. With responsible practices, such as sustainable tourism and cultural education, the benefits of understanding far outweigh the drawbacks. Therefore, I disagree that international tourism primarily creates tension; instead, it has the potential to build bridges between cultures.","annotations":["Balanced argument with clear opinion","Well-developed paragraphs with examples","Sophisticated vocabulary and cohesive devices"]},{"id":"gm-50","type":"speaking","band":"8.0","title":"Describe a book that you found useful","prompt":"Describe a book that you found useful. You should say: what the book is, when you read it, what it was about, and explain why you found it useful.","essay":"I'd like to talk about a book called 'Thinking, Fast and Slow' by Daniel Kahneman. I read it about three years ago when I was preparing for a critical thinking course. It's a non-fiction book that explores the two systems of thought that drive our decision-making: the fast, intuitive system and the slow, rational one.\n\nWhat made it so useful was how it explained common cognitive biases that affect everyday choices. For instance, it discussed the anchoring effect and loss aversion, which I realised I had experienced in my own life. The book provided practical insights into why we make irrational decisions and how to recognise these patterns.\n\nI found it particularly useful because it helped me in both my personal and professional life. At work, I started questioning my initial assumptions and considering alternative perspectives before making decisions. In my personal life, I became more aware of how advertisements and media use these biases to influence me. It also improved my ability to argue logically and avoid fallacies.\n\nOverall, the book was eye-opening. It didn't just present theories; it offered actionable strategies for better thinking. I would highly recommend it to anyone interested in psychology or self-improvement. It's one of those books that stays with you and changes how you view the world.","annotations":["Fluent and coherent with clear structure","Specific details and personal application","Advanced vocabulary and natural expression"]},{"id":"gm-51","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Writing Task 2: Remote Work and Urban Planning","prompt":"Some people believe that the rise of remote work will lead to the decline of city centres, while others think it will revitalise them. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","essay":"The pandemic-induced shift to remote work has sparked debate about the future of urban centres. While some predict their decline, others foresee revitalisation. In my view, city centres will not vanish but will undergo significant transformation, becoming more residential and leisure-oriented.\n\nThose who anticipate decline argue that reduced commuting will empty office districts, hurting businesses that rely on foot traffic. With fewer workers, cafes, shops, and transport services may suffer, leading to economic decay. Moreover, if people can work from anywhere, they might migrate to suburbs or smaller towns, reducing demand for city living. This could create a vicious cycle of falling property values and increased crime, as seen in some American downtowns post-2020.\n\nConversely, optimists contend that remote work can revitalise cities by reducing congestion and pollution, making them more attractive. Former office spaces can be converted into housing, addressing shortages and bringing residents back. Additionally, with fewer daily commuters, city centres could become hubs for culture, entertainment, and social interaction, boosting local economies. For example, Singapore has encouraged the conversion of unused offices into apartments, reinvigorating its central area.\n\nIn my opinion, the outcome depends on policy responses. Cities that adapt by diversifying their economies and improving liveability will thrive. Those that cling to outdated models may decline. Overall, remote work is a catalyst for change, not necessarily decline. With strategic planning, city centres can emerge more resilient and vibrant, catering to residents rather than just workers.","annotations":["Clear position stated in introduction and maintained throughout.","Balanced discussion of both views with relevant examples.","Cohesive devices used effectively (e.g., 'conversely', 'in my opinion')."]},{"id":"gm-52","type":"speaking","band":"7.5","title":"Speaking Part 2: Describe a Skill You Learned","prompt":"Describe a skill you learned that was difficult at first. You should say: what the skill is, when you learned it, how you learned it, and explain why it was difficult and how you felt when you mastered it.","essay":"I'd like to talk about learning to play the guitar, which I started about five years ago. I had always been fascinated by music, and I decided to take it up as a hobby. At first, I thought it would be easy, but it turned out to be quite challenging.\n\nI learned primarily through online tutorials and a lot of practice. I remember spending hours trying to get my fingers to press the strings correctly without buzzing. The chords seemed impossible to master, and my fingertips were sore for weeks. The biggest difficulty was coordinating my left hand on the fretboard with my right hand strumming. It felt like patting my head and rubbing my stomach at the same time. There were moments I wanted to give up, but I was determined.\n\nAfter about six months of consistent practice, things started to click. I could play simple songs, and the soreness turned into calluses. The sense of achievement when I played my first full song for my family was incredible. It boosted my confidence and taught me the value of perseverance. Now, playing the guitar is a way for me to relax and express myself. It was a tough journey, but absolutely worth it.","annotations":["Covers all bullet points in a natural, extended manner.","Uses descriptive language and personal anecdotes effectively.","Shows range of tenses and vocabulary without being overly complex."]},{"id":"gm-53","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Writing Task 1: Bar Chart on Energy Consumption","prompt":"The bar chart below shows the energy consumption in terawatt-hours (TWh) of five countries in 2010 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"The bar chart compares energy consumption in five countries—China, the United States, India, Russia, and Japan—in 2010 and 2020, measured in terawatt-hours (TWh).\n\nOverall, China and the United States were the largest consumers in both years, with China showing the most significant increase. In contrast, Japan and Russia experienced slight declines, while India's consumption grew steadily.\n\nIn 2010, China consumed approximately 3,500 TWh, the highest among the five, followed by the United States at around 3,000 TWh. By 2020, China's consumption had surged to about 5,500 TWh, a rise of over 50%. The United States saw a modest decrease to roughly 2,800 TWh. India's consumption doubled from 500 TWh in 2010 to 1,000 TWh in 2020, overtaking Russia and Japan.\n\nRussia and Japan had relatively lower consumption. Russia started at 800 TWh in 2010 and dropped to 700 TWh in 2020, while Japan decreased from 900 TWh to 800 TWh over the same period. In summary, while China and India increased their energy use, the other three countries either remained stable or reduced consumption, reflecting differing economic and energy policies.","annotations":["Clear overview highlighting main trends.","Accurate data reporting with comparisons.","Logical organisation with cohesive devices."]},{"id":"gm-54","type":"speaking","band":"8.0","title":"Speaking Part 3: The Impact of Technology on Communication","prompt":"In your opinion, has technology improved or harmed interpersonal communication? Support your answer with examples.","essay":"That's an intriguing question. On balance, I believe technology has both improved and harmed interpersonal communication, but the net effect depends on how it's used. On the positive side, technology has made communication more accessible and instantaneous. For instance, video calls allow families separated by continents to maintain close bonds, something unimaginable a few decades ago. Social media platforms enable people to reconnect with old friends and build communities around shared interests. In that sense, technology has expanded our social networks and strengthened long-distance relationships.\n\nHowever, there are significant drawbacks. Face-to-face interactions often suffer as people become glued to their screens. I've noticed in cafes that groups of friends might be physically together but mentally absent, each scrolling through their phones. This can erode the quality of communication, reducing empathy and non-verbal cues. Moreover, the anonymity of online interactions can foster rudeness and misunderstandings.\n\nUltimately, technology is a tool. If used mindfully, it can enhance communication; if abused, it can isolate us. I think the key is to strike a balance, prioritising genuine human connection over digital convenience. So, while there are harms, I lean towards seeing technology as a positive force if we manage it wisely.","annotations":["Balanced viewpoint with clear reasoning.","Provides concrete examples to support arguments.","Sophisticated vocabulary and cohesive flow."]},{"id":"gm-55","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Writing Task 2: The Role of Arts in Education","prompt":"Some people think that arts subjects such as music and drama are essential in schools, while others believe they are a waste of time and resources. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","essay":"The place of arts in the school curriculum is a contentious issue. While some advocate for their essential role in holistic education, others dismiss them as superfluous. I firmly believe that arts subjects are indispensable for fostering creativity and emotional intelligence, though they should not overshadow core academic subjects.\n\nProponents of arts education argue that subjects like music and drama enhance cognitive development, improve motor skills, and boost confidence. Research indicates that students engaged in arts perform better academically and exhibit greater empathy. Moreover, the arts provide an outlet for self-expression, which is crucial for mental well-being. For instance, a child who struggles with mathematics might excel in painting, discovering a talent that builds self-esteem.\n\nOpponents, however, contend that limited school budgets should prioritise STEM subjects, which are seen as more directly linked to employability and economic growth. They argue that arts are leisure activities that can be pursued outside school, and that focusing on them distracts from rigorous academic preparation. In times of fiscal constraints, cutting arts programmes is often an easy decision.\n\nIn my opinion, a balanced curriculum is paramount. Arts should be compulsory up to a certain age to ensure exposure, after which students can specialise. This way, we nurture well-rounded individuals without sacrificing academic excellence. Ultimately, education should prepare students for life, not just work, and the arts are fundamental to that mission.","annotations":["Clear thesis and balanced discussion.","Use of evidence and examples to support points.","Coherent paragraphing and linking words."]},{"id":"gm-56","type":"speaking","band":"7.5","title":"Speaking Part 1: Hometown and Daily Routine","prompt":"Let's talk about your hometown. Where is your hometown? What do you like most about it? And what is your daily routine like?","essay":"My hometown is a medium-sized city called Riverside, located in the eastern part of my country. It's known for its beautiful riverbanks and lush parks, which I absolutely love. What I like most about it is the balance between urban convenience and natural beauty. You can find modern shopping centres but also quiet spots to relax. The people are friendly too, which makes it feel like a big community.\n\nAs for my daily routine, I usually wake up around 6:30 a.m. I start with a quick workout or a jog in the park near my house. Then I have breakfast and head to work or university. I'm usually busy until late afternoon. In the evenings, I like to unwind by reading or watching a series. I also try to cook dinner at home rather than eating out. On weekends, I catch up with friends or explore new cafes. My routine is fairly structured, but I enjoy it because it keeps me productive and healthy. Of course, sometimes I sleep in on Sundays, which is a nice treat.","annotations":["Answers all questions with relevant details.","Natural conversational tone with some idiomatic language.","Uses a range of tenses and sentence structures."]},{"id":"gm-57","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Writing Task 1: Process Diagram of Chocolate Production","prompt":"The diagram illustrates the process of making chocolate from cocoa beans. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features.","essay":"The flowchart depicts the ten-stage process of chocolate production, from harvesting cocoa beans to the final moulding of chocolate bars.\n\nInitially, cocoa beans are harvested from cacao trees and the pods are opened to extract the beans. These beans then undergo fermentation for several days, which develops their flavour. After fermentation, the beans are spread out to dry in the sun. Next, they are roasted at high temperatures to enhance aroma and colour. Following roasting, the beans are winnowed to remove shells, leaving cocoa nibs.\n\nThe nibs are then ground into a thick paste called cocoa liquor. This liquor is pressed to extract cocoa butter, leaving a solid cocoa cake. The cake is pulverised into cocoa powder. To make chocolate, cocoa liquor, cocoa butter, sugar, and sometimes milk are blended. The mixture is then refined and conched to achieve a smooth texture. Finally, the liquid chocolate is tempered, poured into moulds, and cooled to form solid bars.\n\nOverall, the process involves multiple stages of preparation, processing, and refinement, transforming raw cocoa beans into a finished chocolate product.","annotations":["Clear sequencing with time markers.","Accurate description of each stage.","Concise overview and logical progression."]},{"id":"gm-58","type":"speaking","band":"8.0","title":"Speaking Part 2: Describe a Memorable Journey","prompt":"Describe a journey you remember well. You should say: where you went, how you travelled, why you remember it, and explain how you felt during the journey.","essay":"I'd like to talk about a memorable journey I took to the Himalayas a couple of years ago. I travelled with two close friends, and we went trekking in the mountains of northern India. We flew to Delhi and then took a train to a small town called Manali, from where we started our trek.\n\nWhat made this journey unforgettable was the sheer beauty of the landscape and the challenges we faced. We trekked for about ten days, crossing high passes and staying in remote villages. The altitude made breathing difficult, and there were moments when I doubted I could continue. But the camaraderie and the stunning views kept me going. I remember one sunrise over the snow-capped peaks that was so breathtaking it brought tears to my eyes.\n\nEmotionally, it was a rollercoaster. I felt exhilarated, exhausted, and sometimes scared, but ultimately a profound sense of accomplishment. The journey taught me resilience and the importance of teamwork. It also deepened my appreciation for nature. Even now, whenever I look at photos from that trip, I'm transported back to those majestic mountains and the feeling of freedom. It was truly a life-changing experience.","annotations":["Rich descriptive language and vivid imagery.","Covers all bullet points naturally.","Shows a range of emotions and reflective insight."]},{"id":"gm-59","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Writing Task 2: Should Governments Tax Unhealthy Foods?","prompt":"Some governments have imposed taxes on unhealthy foods and drinks to improve public health. To what extent is this an effective measure? What other measures could be taken?","essay":"In response to rising obesity rates, several governments have introduced taxes on sugary drinks and junk food. While such taxes can be effective, they are not a panacea. I believe they should be part of a broader strategy that includes education and regulation.\n\nTaxing unhealthy products can reduce consumption by making them more expensive. Evidence from Mexico and the UK shows that sugar taxes led to a decline in purchases of sugary drinks. The revenue generated can also fund health campaigns. However, these taxes are regressive, disproportionately affecting low-income households, and may not change long-term habits if healthier alternatives are not affordable or accessible. Moreover, the food industry often pushes back, and consumers might simply switch to other unhealthy options.\n\nTo effectively tackle poor diets, governments should implement complementary measures. First, subsidise healthy foods like fruits and vegetables to make them more affordable. Second, mandate clear nutritional labelling and restrict advertising of junk food to children. Third, invest in public health education to raise awareness about diet-related diseases. Fourth, promote urban planning that encourages physical activity, such as bike lanes and parks. Finally, collaborate with food manufacturers to reformulate products with less sugar, salt, and fat.\n\nIn conclusion, while taxes on unhealthy foods can be a useful tool, they are insufficient alone. A comprehensive approach that addresses affordability, education, and environmental factors is essential to improve public health sustainably.","annotations":["Clear position and thorough exploration of the issue.","Provides multiple measures with logical justification.","Cohesive devices and sophisticated vocabulary."]},{"id":"gm-60","type":"speaking","band":"7.5","title":"Speaking Part 3: The Value of Cultural Heritage","prompt":"Why do you think it is important to preserve cultural heritage? How can governments and individuals contribute?","essay":"I believe preserving cultural heritage is crucial for several reasons. Firstly, it provides a sense of identity and continuity. Knowing where we come from helps us understand who we are. For example, traditional festivals, languages, and crafts connect us to our ancestors and give communities a shared narrative. Secondly, cultural heritage is a source of tourism and economic benefit. Historic sites and museums attract visitors, creating jobs and revenue. Thirdly, it fosters mutual respect and understanding between different cultures, which is vital in our globalised world.\n\nGovernments can contribute by funding restoration projects, enacting laws to protect heritage sites, and integrating cultural education into school curricula. They can also support artisans and performers through grants and subsidies. Individuals, on the other hand, can visit museums, participate in cultural events, and pass on traditions to younger generations. Social media can be used to raise awareness and celebrate diversity. In my view, it's a shared responsibility. If we neglect our heritage, we risk losing a part of ourselves. So, both top-down and bottom-up efforts are necessary to safeguard it for future generations.","annotations":["Well-structured response with clear reasons.","Provides roles for both governments and individuals.","Appropriate vocabulary and cohesive linking."]},{"id":"gm-61","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Writing Task 2 - Remote Work","prompt":"Some people believe that remote work is beneficial for both employers and employees, while others think it has more drawbacks. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","essay":"The shift towards remote work has sparked a debate about its advantages and disadvantages. While some argue that it benefits both employers and employees, others contend that it has significant drawbacks. This essay will examine both perspectives before offering my own view.\n\nOn the one hand, remote work offers flexibility and cost savings. Employees can avoid commuting, leading to better work-life balance and reduced expenses. Employers, meanwhile, can save on office space and access a global talent pool. For instance, many tech companies have reported increased productivity and employee satisfaction after transitioning to remote setups.\n\nOn the other hand, critics highlight the lack of social interaction and potential for isolation. Without face-to-face communication, teamwork and creativity may suffer. Additionally, employees might struggle to disconnect from work, leading to burnout. Employers may also find it challenging to monitor performance and maintain company culture.\n\nIn my opinion, while remote work has challenges, its benefits outweigh the drawbacks, especially when implemented with clear policies and support. A hybrid model, combining remote and office work, can mitigate isolation while retaining flexibility. Ultimately, the success of remote work depends on the nature of the job and the willingness of both parties to adapt.","annotations":["Clear discussion of both views and a well-defined opinion.","Effective use of cohesive devices (on the one hand, on the other hand).","Range of vocabulary and complex sentence structures."]},{"id":"gm-62","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Writing Task 2 - University Education","prompt":"Some people think that universities should provide graduates with the knowledge and skills needed in the workplace. Others think that the true function of a university should be to give access to knowledge for its own sake. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","essay":"The role of universities has long been debated: should they focus on vocational training or on the pursuit of knowledge for its own sake? This essay will discuss both perspectives before presenting my own opinion.\n\nThose who advocate for practical education argue that universities should equip students with job-ready skills. In a competitive job market, graduates need specific competencies to secure employment. For example, engineering and business programs often include internships and practical projects that directly enhance employability. This approach benefits both individuals and the economy by reducing unemployment and filling skill gaps.\n\nConversely, supporters of liberal education believe that universities should foster critical thinking and intellectual curiosity. They argue that education is not merely a means to an end but a valuable end in itself. Subjects like philosophy, literature, and history enrich students' understanding of the world and cultivate lifelong learning. Moreover, a broad knowledge base can adapt to changing career demands, as specific skills may become obsolete.\n\nIn my view, a balanced approach is ideal. Universities should offer both vocational and theoretical courses, allowing students to choose based on their goals. However, the primary aim should be to develop well-rounded individuals who can think critically and contribute to society, not just to the economy.","annotations":["Balanced discussion with a clear personal stance.","Good use of examples to support arguments.","Varied sentence structures and appropriate linking words."]},{"id":"gm-63","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Writing Task 1 - Line Graph","prompt":"The graph below shows the average monthly temperatures in three cities over a year. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"The line graph illustrates the average monthly temperatures in three cities—London, Singapore, and Moscow—over a one-year period.\n\nOverall, Singapore experiences consistently high temperatures throughout the year, while London and Moscow show significant seasonal variations. Moscow has the most extreme range, with very cold winters and mild summers.\n\nIn Singapore, temperatures remain stable, fluctuating between 26°C and 28°C, with little variation across the months. In contrast, London's temperatures range from around 5°C in January to 23°C in July. Moscow exhibits the greatest fluctuation, starting at approximately -10°C in January, rising to 19°C in July, and then dropping again.\n\nA comparison of the three cities reveals that Singapore is the warmest year-round, whereas Moscow is the coldest in winter. London's temperatures are moderate, never reaching the extremes of the other two. Interestingly, during summer months, London and Moscow have similar temperatures, but in winter, Moscow is significantly colder. The data indicates that proximity to the equator and continental climate play key roles in these patterns.","annotations":["Clear overview and comparison of main features.","Accurate data reporting with appropriate vocabulary.","Good use of cohesive devices to link ideas."]},{"id":"gm-64","type":"speaking","band":"7.5","title":"Speaking Part 2 - A Memorable Journey","prompt":"Describe a memorable journey you have taken. You should say: where you went, who you went with, what you did, and explain why it was memorable.","essay":"I'd like to talk about a memorable journey I took to Japan a couple of years ago. I went with my best friend from university, and we spent two weeks traveling around the country. We started in Tokyo, then took the bullet train to Kyoto and Osaka.\n\nIn Tokyo, we explored the bustling streets of Shibuya and visited ancient temples like Senso-ji. The contrast between modern skyscrapers and traditional shrines was fascinating. In Kyoto, we immersed ourselves in the serene atmosphere of bamboo forests and participated in a tea ceremony. We also tried local cuisine, from sushi to ramen, which was an adventure in itself.\n\nWhat made this journey so memorable was not just the places we saw, but the cultural immersion and the time spent with my friend. We navigated language barriers together, got lost in unfamiliar neighborhoods, and laughed through it all. It was a journey that broadened my perspective and strengthened our friendship. I think it was memorable because it combined adventure, learning, and companionship in a way that few other experiences have.","annotations":["Covers all bullet points with rich details.","Varied vocabulary and natural expressions.","Clear explanation of why it was memorable."]},{"id":"gm-65","type":"speaking","band":"8.0","title":"Speaking Part 3 - Technology and Communication","prompt":"How has technology changed the way people communicate? Do you think these changes are positive or negative?","essay":"Technology has revolutionized communication in profound ways. In the past, people relied on letters or face-to-face meetings, which could take days or even weeks. Now, with smartphones and the internet, we can instantly connect with anyone anywhere. Social media platforms, messaging apps, and video calls have made communication faster and more convenient.\n\nHowever, these changes have both positive and negative aspects. On the positive side, technology has brought people closer, enabling long-distance relationships and global collaboration. It has also democratized information, allowing anyone to share ideas. On the negative side, some argue that digital communication lacks the depth of face-to-face interaction, leading to misunderstandings or superficial relationships. There's also the issue of privacy and the pressure to be constantly available.\n\nPersonally, I believe the changes are largely positive, but we need to use technology mindfully. It's important to balance online interactions with in-person connections to maintain meaningful relationships. Overall, technology has enhanced communication, but it's up to us to ensure it doesn't replace genuine human contact.","annotations":["Well-developed arguments with clear examples.","Balanced view with a personal conclusion.","Advanced vocabulary and fluent delivery."]},{"id":"gm-66","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Writing Task 2 - Environmental Responsibility","prompt":"Some people believe that individuals can make a difference in protecting the environment, while others think that only governments and large companies can make a real impact. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","essay":"Environmental protection is a pressing global issue. While some argue that individual actions can make a difference, others believe that only governments and large corporations have the power to effect real change. This essay will discuss both perspectives and provide my own opinion.\n\nOn the one hand, individuals can contribute by adopting eco-friendly habits such as recycling, reducing energy consumption, and using public transport. These small actions, when multiplied across millions of people, can significantly reduce carbon footprints. For example, if everyone in a city recycled, the impact on landfill waste would be substantial. Moreover, individuals can influence others and drive demand for sustainable products, pressuring companies to change.\n\nOn the other hand, governments and large companies have the resources and authority to implement large-scale changes. Governments can enact laws, impose carbon taxes, and invest in renewable energy. Companies can adopt sustainable practices and reduce industrial pollution. Without their cooperation, individual efforts may be insufficient. For instance, a single factory can emit more pollution than thousands of individuals.\n\nIn my opinion, both are essential. Individuals can create a cultural shift, but systemic change requires government and corporate action. Therefore, a combined approach is necessary to effectively address environmental challenges.","annotations":["Clear discussion of both sides with relevant examples.","Coherent paragraphing and linking devices.","Personal opinion is well-integrated and justified."]},{"id":"gm-67","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Writing Task 1 - Bar Chart","prompt":"The bar chart below shows the percentage of people in different age groups who use social media in a European country. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"The bar chart compares the percentage of social media users across five age groups in a European country.\n\nOverall, social media usage is highest among younger people and declines progressively with age. The 18-24 age group has the highest usage, with over 90% using social media, while the 65+ group has the lowest, at around 30%.\n\nLooking at the data, the 18-24 and 25-34 age groups show similar high levels, both exceeding 85%. Usage drops slightly for the 35-44 group to approximately 70%, and then more sharply for the 45-54 group to about 50%. The 55-64 group has around 40% usage, and the oldest group, 65+, has the lowest at 30%.\n\nA clear inverse relationship between age and social media usage is evident. The younger the age group, the more likely they are to use social media. This pattern suggests that digital literacy and familiarity with technology decrease with age, or that older generations prefer traditional communication methods. The chart highlights a significant generational divide in social media adoption.","annotations":["Clear overview and logical grouping of data.","Precise language for describing trends and comparisons.","Good use of cohesive devices to link sentences."]},{"id":"gm-68","type":"speaking","band":"7.5","title":"Speaking Part 1 - Hometown","prompt":"Where is your hometown? What do you like most about it? Would you like to live there in the future?","essay":"My hometown is a small coastal city in the south of my country. It's known for its beautiful beaches and relaxed atmosphere. I lived there until I moved to the capital for university.\n\nWhat I like most about it is the sense of community. Everyone knows each other, and there's a strong local culture. The seafood is amazing, and I love walking along the promenade at sunset. It's a very peaceful place, which is a nice contrast to the busy city where I live now.\n\nAs for the future, I'm not sure if I'd like to live there permanently. While it's a wonderful place to visit and relax, career opportunities are limited. I might consider returning later in life when I want a quieter pace. For now, I enjoy visiting during holidays. So, I'd say it's a great place to grow up and retire, but maybe not for the middle part of life.","annotations":["Answers all parts of the question naturally.","Uses descriptive language and personal examples.","Shows ability to express uncertainty and future plans."]},{"id":"gm-69","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Writing Task 2 - Online Education","prompt":"Some people think that online education is as effective as traditional classroom education, while others disagree. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","essay":"The rise of online education has prompted a debate about its effectiveness compared to traditional classroom learning. While some believe it is equally effective, others argue that it falls short. This essay will discuss both views and provide my own opinion.\n\nOn the one hand, online education offers flexibility and accessibility. Students can learn at their own pace and from anywhere, which is particularly beneficial for those with jobs or disabilities. Moreover, online platforms often provide a wide range of resources, such as videos and interactive quizzes, which can enhance learning. For example, many universities now offer online courses that are just as rigorous as on-campus ones.\n\nOn the other hand, traditional classroom education provides face-to-face interaction, which many argue is crucial for effective learning. In a classroom, students can ask questions immediately, engage in discussions, and receive instant feedback. Additionally, the structured environment helps maintain discipline and motivation. Some subjects, like laboratory sciences, require hands-on experience that online learning cannot replicate.\n\nIn my opinion, while online education is valuable, it cannot fully replace traditional classrooms. A blended approach, combining the best of both, may be the most effective. Ultimately, the effectiveness depends on the subject, the learner's style, and the quality of instruction.","annotations":["Balanced discussion with clear examples.","Coherent structure and paragraphing.","Personal opinion is clearly stated and supported."]},{"id":"gm-70","type":"speaking","band":"8.0","title":"Speaking Part 2 - A Skill You Learned","prompt":"Describe a skill you learned that was difficult at first but became easier with practice. You should say: what the skill was, how you learned it, why it was difficult, and how you feel about it now.","essay":"I'd like to talk about learning to play the guitar, which was quite challenging initially but became enjoyable with practice. I started learning about three years ago when I bought a second-hand acoustic guitar. I taught myself using online tutorials and apps, practicing whenever I had free time.\n\nAt first, it was incredibly difficult. My fingers hurt from pressing the strings, and coordinating my hands to strum and fret seemed impossible. I often felt frustrated and wanted to give up. However, I persisted, practicing for just 15 minutes a day. Gradually, my fingers developed calluses, and muscle memory kicked in. After a few months, I could play simple songs, and the sense of accomplishment was immense.\n\nNow, I feel proud of my progress. Playing guitar has become a way to relax and express myself. It taught me patience and the value of consistent effort. I believe that any skill, no matter how hard it seems at first, can be mastered with dedication and practice.","annotations":["Covers all bullet points with a clear narrative.","Uses varied vocabulary and idiomatic expressions.","Reflects on personal growth and feelings effectively."]},{"id":"gm-71","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Writing Task 2: University Education and Employment","prompt":"Some people believe that university education should be free for all students, while others argue that students should pay for their own tuition. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","essay":"The question of whether higher education should be funded by the government or by students themselves is a contentious issue. While some advocate for free university access, others insist on tuition fees. This essay will examine both perspectives before offering my own view.\n\nOn the one hand, making university free can bring significant societal benefits. It ensures that talented individuals from low-income backgrounds are not excluded due to financial constraints, thereby promoting social mobility and equality. Moreover, a well-educated workforce drives economic growth and innovation. For instance, countries like Germany have abolished tuition fees and have seen a rise in skilled graduates contributing to the economy.\n\nOn the other hand, opponents argue that free education places a heavy burden on taxpayers and may lead to overcrowded institutions. They contend that students who benefit directly from their degrees should bear part of the cost. Additionally, tuition fees can be used to improve the quality of education and facilities. In the UK, for example, fees have helped universities maintain high standards, although they have also sparked debates about debt.\n\nIn my opinion, a balanced approach is preferable. While free education is ideal, it is not always feasible. A system of income-contingent loans, where graduates repay only when they earn above a certain threshold, can ensure access without overburdening public finances. This way, both individual and societal interests are protected.\n\nIn conclusion, although free university education has merits, a mixed funding model that combines government support with reasonable student contributions is more sustainable and equitable.","annotations":["Clear thesis statement and balanced discussion.","Use of examples (Germany, UK) to support arguments.","Cohesive devices like 'on the one hand' and 'in conclusion'."]},{"id":"gm-72","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Writing Task 2: Environmental Responsibility","prompt":"Some people think that environmental problems should be solved by increasing the cost of fuel for cars and other vehicles. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","essay":"The proposal to combat environmental degradation by raising fuel prices is a controversial one. While such a measure could potentially reduce emissions, I largely disagree with this approach, as it may cause significant economic hardship and overlooks broader solutions.\n\nAdmittedly, higher fuel costs could discourage excessive driving and encourage the use of public transport or electric vehicles. This might lead to a reduction in carbon emissions in the short term. However, this policy is regressive, disproportionately affecting low-income households who rely on cars for work and daily activities. In many rural areas, public transport is inadequate, leaving residents with no viable alternative. Thus, instead of curbing pollution, it could deepen social inequality.\n\nFurthermore, focusing solely on fuel prices fails to address the root causes of environmental damage. Industrial emissions, deforestation, and waste management are equally significant contributors. A more effective strategy would involve investing in renewable energy, improving public transit infrastructure, and implementing carbon pricing for large corporations. For instance, countries like Sweden have successfully reduced emissions through a combination of carbon taxes and green incentives, rather than merely penalising drivers.\n\nIn conclusion, while increasing fuel costs might have a marginal impact on pollution, it is an inequitable and insufficient solution. Governments should adopt comprehensive policies that target all major sources of emissions and support sustainable alternatives. Only through such holistic measures can we achieve lasting environmental progress.","annotations":["Strong position stated clearly in introduction.","Counter-argument acknowledged then refuted.","Use of specific policy examples (Sweden) to strengthen argument."]},{"id":"gm-73","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Writing Task 1: Bar Chart on Household Expenditure","prompt":"The bar chart below shows the average monthly expenditure of households in three countries (A, B, and C) on five categories: housing, food, transportation, healthcare, and education. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"The bar chart compares the average monthly spending of households in three countries on five key categories: housing, food, transportation, healthcare, and education.\n\nOverall, housing is the largest expense in all three countries, while healthcare and education receive the least allocation. Country A spends the most on housing, whereas Country C spends the least.\n\nIn Country A, housing accounts for approximately $1,200 per month, significantly higher than in Country B ($900) and Country C ($700). Food expenditure follows a similar pattern, with Country A spending $600, Country B $500, and Country C $400. Transportation costs are relatively consistent across the three, ranging from $300 to $350.\n\nHealthcare and education show lower figures. Country A spends $200 on healthcare and $150 on education, while Country B spends $180 and $120 respectively. Country C has the lowest expenditure in both categories, at $150 for healthcare and $100 for education.\n\nIn summary, Country A has the highest overall spending, particularly on housing and food, while Country C is the most frugal. The disparities are most pronounced in housing, whereas transportation costs are more uniform across the three nations.","annotations":["Clear overview paragraph summarising main trends.","Accurate data reporting with comparisons.","Varied sentence structures and linking words."]},{"id":"gm-74","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Writing Task 1: Line Graph on Internet Usage","prompt":"The line graph below shows the percentage of internet users in four different countries between 2000 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"The line graph illustrates the proportion of the population using the internet in four countries—namely A, B, C, and D—over a 20-year period from 2000 to 2020.\n\nOverall, all four countries experienced a significant increase in internet usage, with Country A consistently maintaining the highest percentage and Country D the lowest. The most dramatic growth occurred in Country C.\n\nIn 2000, Country A had the highest usage at 50%, followed by Country B at 30%, Country C at 10%, and Country D at just 5%. By 2010, Country A had risen to 75%, while Country B reached 60%. Country C saw a sharp rise to 40%, and Country D climbed to 20%. The gap between the highest and lowest remained substantial.\n\nBy 2020, Country A had nearly saturated the market at 95%. Country B also showed robust growth, reaching 85%. Country C continued its upward trajectory, hitting 70%, which represents the most rapid increase over the period. Country D, despite doubling its usage from 2000, remained the lowest at 45%. Notably, the rate of growth in Country C accelerated after 2010, suggesting a late but rapid adoption of the internet.\n\nIn conclusion, while all countries made progress, the disparities in internet penetration persisted, with Country A leading and Country D lagging behind.","annotations":["Well-structured with overview and detailed paragraphs.","Effective use of data points and comparisons.","Range of vocabulary for describing trends (saturated, robust, trajectory)."]},{"id":"gm-75","type":"speaking","band":"7.5","title":"Speaking Part 2: Describe a Skill You Learned","prompt":"Describe a skill you learned that you find useful. You should say: what the skill is, when and where you learned it, how you learned it, and explain why you find it useful.","essay":"I'd like to talk about learning to cook, which I picked up during my first year at university. I moved away from home and realized I couldn't rely on fast food forever, so I decided to teach myself some basic recipes. I started by watching YouTube tutorials and following simple recipes, like pasta and stir-fries. At first, I made plenty of mistakes—burning rice or overseasoning dishes—but with practice, I improved. I also asked my grandmother for tips over the phone, which was a nice way to connect with her. Now, I can prepare a variety of meals, and I even enjoy experimenting with new ingredients. I find this skill incredibly useful because it saves money, helps me eat healthier, and allows me to host friends for dinner. It's also a creative outlet and a way to relax after a long day. Cooking has become an essential part of my daily routine, and I'm proud of how far I've come.","annotations":["Covers all bullet points clearly.","Natural flow with personal anecdotes.","Varied vocabulary and grammatical structures."]},{"id":"gm-76","type":"speaking","band":"8.0","title":"Speaking Part 3: Discussion on Technology and Communication","prompt":"Discuss how technology has changed the way people communicate. Do you think these changes are positive or negative?","essay":"Technology has revolutionised communication in profound ways. In the past, people relied on letters or landline phones, which were often slow and limited. Now, with smartphones and social media, we can instantly connect with anyone, anywhere. This immediacy has both advantages and drawbacks. On the positive side, it has made the world more interconnected, enabling people to maintain long-distance relationships, collaborate globally, and access information rapidly. For instance, video calls allow families separated by continents to see each other regularly. However, there are negatives. Face-to-face interactions have declined, and some argue that digital communication lacks the depth and emotional nuance of in-person conversations. Moreover, the constant connectivity can lead to stress and a sense of always being 'on call'. Social media, while connecting us, can also foster superficial relationships and contribute to feelings of isolation. Overall, I believe the changes are largely positive, but we must be mindful of the downsides. Striking a balance between online and offline communication is crucial. Technology should enhance, not replace, genuine human connection.","annotations":["Balanced discussion of advantages and disadvantages.","Use of specific examples to illustrate points.","Clear personal opinion expressed with nuance."]},{"id":"gm-77","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Writing Task 2: Work-Life Balance","prompt":"In many countries, people are working longer hours and having less leisure time. What are the causes of this? What solutions can you suggest?","essay":"In recent decades, there has been a noticeable trend towards longer working hours and reduced leisure time in many parts of the world. This essay will explore the primary causes of this phenomenon and propose some viable solutions.\n\nOne major cause is the advancement of technology, which has blurred the boundaries between work and personal life. With smartphones and laptops, employees are expected to be available at all times, leading to an 'always-on' culture. Another factor is the competitive nature of the modern economy. Many workers fear losing their jobs or missing promotions, so they put in extra hours to prove their dedication. Additionally, the rising cost of living forces individuals to take on additional work or overtime to make ends meet.\n\nTo address this issue, several measures can be taken. Firstly, governments should enforce stricter labour laws that limit working hours and guarantee paid leave. For example, some European countries have implemented a maximum 48-hour workweek. Secondly, companies should foster a culture that values productivity over mere presence. Encouraging employees to disconnect after work hours can improve mental health and overall efficiency. Finally, individuals can set boundaries, such as not checking emails after a certain time, to protect their personal time.\n\nIn conclusion, while technology and economic pressures drive longer hours, a combination of legislative, corporate, and personal changes can help restore a healthier work-life balance.","annotations":["Clear cause-and-effect structure.","Specific solutions with examples.","Formal tone and cohesive linking."]},{"id":"gm-78","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Writing Task 1: Process Diagram on Chocolate Production","prompt":"The diagram below shows the process of making chocolate from cocoa beans. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features.","essay":"The diagram illustrates the various stages involved in the production of chocolate, from the harvesting of cocoa beans to the final product.\n\nOverall, the process consists of approximately ten main steps, beginning with farming and ending with the manufacture of chocolate bars. It is a linear process that requires several mechanical and chemical treatments.\n\nInitially, cocoa beans are harvested from cocoa trees, after which they are fermented and dried in the sun. Once dried, the beans are transported to a factory, where they are roasted at high temperatures. This roasting process enhances the flavour and makes the beans easier to crack. Next, the roasted beans are winnowed to remove their shells, leaving behind cocoa nibs. These nibs are then ground into a thick paste known as cocoa liquor.\n\nThe liquor is subsequently pressed to extract cocoa butter and cocoa powder. The cocoa butter is mixed with other ingredients, such as sugar and milk, to create chocolate liquor. This mixture is then refined and conched to achieve a smooth texture. Finally, the chocolate is tempered, moulded into bars, and packaged for distribution. In summary, chocolate production is a multi-stage process that transforms raw cocoa beans into a refined consumer product.","annotations":["Clear overview of the process stages.","Sequential markers (initially, next, finally) guide the reader.","Precise vocabulary for describing processes."]},{"id":"gm-79","type":"speaking","band":"7.5","title":"Speaking Part 1: Hometown and Daily Routine","prompt":"Answer the following questions: Where is your hometown? What do you like most about it? What is your daily routine like?","essay":"My hometown is a small coastal city in the south of my country. It's known for its beautiful beaches and relaxed atmosphere. What I like most about it is the sense of community; everyone knows each other, and there are frequent local festivals. The pace of life is slower than in big cities, which I appreciate. As for my daily routine, I usually wake up around 6:30 a.m. and go for a short jog along the promenade. After that, I have breakfast and start work at 9 a.m. I work as a graphic designer, so I spend most of my day at the computer. In the afternoon, I take a break to walk my dog, and then I continue working until about 6 p.m. Evenings are for relaxing—I often cook dinner, read a book, or meet friends at a café. On weekends, I like to explore nearby hiking trails or visit my family. My routine is fairly structured, but I try to keep it flexible to avoid monotony.","annotations":["Answers all questions fully and naturally.","Use of descriptive language and specific details.","Good range of tenses and sentence structures."]},{"id":"gm-80","type":"speaking","band":"8.0","title":"Speaking Part 2: Describe a Memorable Journey","prompt":"Describe a memorable journey you have taken. You should say: where you went, who you went with, what you did, and explain why it was memorable.","essay":"One of the most memorable journeys I've taken was a trip to Japan a couple of years ago. I went with my best friend from university, and we spent two weeks travelling from Tokyo to Kyoto and then down to Hiroshima. We immersed ourselves in the culture—visiting ancient temples, trying local cuisine like sushi and ramen, and even staying in a traditional ryokan. What made it truly memorable, though, was the people we met. A local family in Kyoto invited us to a tea ceremony, and despite the language barrier, we shared stories and laughter. The journey also challenged me personally; navigating the complex train system and communicating without knowing Japanese pushed me out of my comfort zone. Additionally, the cherry blossoms were in full bloom, creating breathtaking scenery. This trip was memorable not just because of the sights, but because it deepened my friendship and gave me a new perspective on life. It taught me the value of stepping outside my routine and embracing the unknown.","annotations":["Rich descriptive details and personal reflection.","Covers all bullet points with elaboration.","Fluent and expressive language with varied vocabulary."]},{"id":"gm-81","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Writing Task 2: Remote Work and City Planning","prompt":"Some people believe that the rise of remote work will lead to the decline of city centres. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","essay":"The pandemic-induced shift to remote work has prompted speculation about the future of urban centres. While some argue that city centres will decline as workers no longer commute daily, I believe they will adapt and remain vibrant, albeit with a different character.\n\nAdmittedly, reduced commuting could decrease demand for office space and the ancillary services that cater to office workers, such as cafes and dry cleaners. This might lead to lower footfall in some business districts. However, city centres are not solely reliant on office workers. They are hubs of culture, entertainment, and retail, attracting visitors for reasons beyond employment. For instance, theatres, museums, and restaurants draw people regardless of where they work.\n\nMoreover, remote work may actually revitalise residential areas within city centres. As people spend more time at home, they may seek larger living spaces, leading to increased demand for housing in urban cores. This could foster mixed-use developments where residential, commercial, and recreational spaces coexist, creating more dynamic communities. Cities like Paris and New York have already seen a surge in residential interest in central neighbourhoods.\n\nFurthermore, local governments can play a pivotal role in shaping this transition. By incentivising the conversion of vacant offices into apartments and supporting small businesses, they can ensure that city centres evolve rather than decline. Such policies have been implemented in cities like Calgary, where office-to-residential conversions are underway.\n\nIn conclusion, while remote work may reduce the traditional nine-to-five bustle, city centres will likely transform into more multifaceted environments. With strategic planning, they can remain the heart of economic and social life.","annotations":["Clear position stated in introduction and maintained throughout.","Balanced consideration of counter-argument before refuting it.","Specific examples (Paris, New York, Calgary) strengthen the argument."]},{"id":"gm-82","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Writing Task 2: Banning Plastic Bags","prompt":"Many countries have banned single-use plastic bags to reduce environmental damage. Do the advantages of such bans outweigh the disadvantages?","essay":"In recent years, numerous countries have implemented bans on single-use plastic bags to mitigate environmental pollution. While these bans have some drawbacks, I believe their advantages are more significant.\n\nThe primary benefit of banning plastic bags is the reduction of plastic waste. Plastic bags take hundreds of years to decompose and often end up in oceans, harming marine life. By eliminating them, countries can drastically cut down on pollution. For example, after Rwanda banned plastic bags in 2008, the country saw a noticeable decrease in litter and improved overall cleanliness. Additionally, such bans encourage the use of reusable bags, fostering a more sustainable mindset among consumers.\n\nHowever, there are disadvantages. Consumers may face inconvenience and additional costs if they forget to bring reusable bags and must purchase them. Small businesses might struggle with the transition, as alternative packaging can be more expensive. In some cases, plastic bag bans have led to an increase in the use of paper bags, which also have environmental costs, such as higher water and energy consumption during production.\n\nDespite these drawbacks, the long-term environmental benefits outweigh the short-term inconveniences. Governments can mitigate negative effects by subsidising reusable bags and educating the public. Moreover, innovation in biodegradable materials can provide viable alternatives. Countries like Kenya have shown that with proper enforcement and public awareness, plastic bag bans can be effective.\n\nIn conclusion, while plastic bag bans may cause temporary inconvenience, they are a crucial step towards reducing plastic pollution. The environmental gains far exceed the disadvantages, making such bans a worthwhile policy.","annotations":["Clear thesis and conclusion that directly answer the question.","Acknowledges counterarguments but explains why they are outweighed.","Uses specific country examples (Rwanda, Kenya) effectively."]},{"id":"gm-83","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Writing Task 1: Line Graph on Coffee Consumption","prompt":"The line graph below shows the average monthly consumption of coffee in three countries (Country A, Country B, and Country C) between 2000 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"The line graph illustrates the average monthly coffee consumption in three countries over a 20-year period from 2000 to 2020.\n\nOverall, Country A consistently had the highest consumption, while Country C had the lowest. However, Country B experienced the most significant growth, nearly tripling its consumption over the period.\n\nIn 2000, Country A consumed approximately 8 kilograms per person per month, far exceeding Country B (2 kg) and Country C (1 kg). By 2010, Country A's consumption had risen steadily to 10 kg, before plateauing and remaining stable until 2020. In contrast, Country B started at 2 kg in 2000 and saw a gradual increase to 4 kg by 2010, followed by a sharp rise to 6 kg by 2015 and reaching 7 kg in 2020. Country C, meanwhile, showed a slow and steady increase from 1 kg in 2000 to 2 kg in 2010, and then to 3 kg by 2020.\n\nIt is notable that while Country A maintained its leading position, the gap between Country A and Country B narrowed considerably over time. By 2020, the difference was only 3 kg, compared to 6 kg in 2000. Country C remained the lowest throughout, but its consumption doubled over the two decades.\n\nIn summary, coffee consumption increased in all three countries, with Country B showing the most dramatic rise, while Country A remained the highest consumer despite a levelling off after 2010.","annotations":["Clear overview paragraph highlighting main trends.","Accurate data reporting with specific figures and dates.","Effective comparison between countries and time periods."]},{"id":"gm-84","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Writing Task 1: Bar Chart on University Enrollment","prompt":"The bar chart below shows the number of students enrolled in four different faculties (Engineering, Business, Arts, and Science) at a university in 2010 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"The bar chart compares student enrollment in four faculties—Engineering, Business, Arts, and Science—at a particular university in 2010 and 2020.\n\nOverall, Business was the most popular faculty in both years, while Arts had the lowest enrollment. However, Engineering saw the most significant growth over the decade.\n\nIn 2010, Business had the highest enrollment with 5,000 students, followed by Engineering with 3,000, Science with 2,500, and Arts with 1,500. By 2020, Business enrollment had increased to 6,000, maintaining its top position. Engineering experienced a substantial rise, reaching 5,000 students, nearly doubling its 2010 figure. Science also grew, though more modestly, to 3,000 students. In contrast, Arts enrollment declined slightly to 1,200 students.\n\nThe most notable change was in Engineering, which overtook Science to become the second most popular faculty. The gap between Business and Engineering narrowed from 2,000 students in 2010 to just 1,000 in 2020. Meanwhile, the difference between Science and Arts widened, with Science enrolling 1,800 more students than Arts in 2020, compared to a 1,000 difference in 2010.\n\nIn summary, while Business remained the leading faculty, Engineering showed the most dynamic growth, and Arts experienced a decline, highlighting a shift towards more technical and business-oriented fields.","annotations":["Clear overview identifying main trends and exceptions.","Precise data selection and comparison across faculties.","Good use of cohesive devices to link ideas."]},{"id":"gm-85","type":"speaking","band":"8.0","title":"Speaking Part 2: Describe a Skill You Learned","prompt":"Describe a skill you learned that you find useful. You should say: what the skill is, when and how you learned it, how you use it, and explain why you find it useful.","essay":"I'd like to talk about learning to code, specifically Python, which I picked up about three years ago during my final year at university. I initially started because I needed to analyse data for my dissertation, and I quickly realised how powerful programming could be. I learned through a combination of online courses, like those on Coursera, and a lot of trial and error. It was challenging at first, but I persevered.\n\nNow, I use coding almost daily in my job as a marketing analyst. I write scripts to automate data collection, clean large datasets, and create visualisations. It's incredibly useful because it saves me hours of manual work. For instance, what used to take a full day to compile now takes just a few minutes. Beyond work, I've also used Python to build a simple personal finance tracker, which helps me manage my budget.\n\nThe reason I find this skill so valuable is that it's versatile and in high demand. It's not just about writing code; it's about problem-solving and logical thinking. It has opened up career opportunities and made me more efficient. Moreover, it's a skill that I can continually improve, which keeps me engaged. I'd recommend it to anyone willing to invest the time.","annotations":["Fluent and coherent with natural fillers and connectors.","Provides specific examples of how the skill is used.","Shows enthusiasm and reflection on the skill's value."]},{"id":"gm-86","type":"speaking","band":"7.5","title":"Speaking Part 3: The Impact of Technology on Communication","prompt":"How has technology changed the way people communicate? Do you think these changes are positive or negative?","essay":"Technology has revolutionised communication in countless ways. In the past, people relied on letters or landline phones, which were slower and less convenient. Now, with smartphones and the internet, we can instantly connect with anyone, anywhere, through messaging apps, video calls, and social media. This immediacy has made the world feel smaller and more connected.\n\nHowever, I think these changes have both positive and negative aspects. On the positive side, technology allows us to maintain long-distance relationships more easily. For example, I can video call my family overseas and see their faces, which was impossible decades ago. It also facilitates quick information sharing, which is crucial in emergencies or business.\n\nOn the negative side, face-to-face interaction has declined. People often prioritise texting over meeting in person, which can weaken personal bonds. There's also the issue of misinformation spreading rapidly through social media. Additionally, constant connectivity can lead to stress and a lack of privacy.\n\nOverall, I believe the benefits outweigh the drawbacks, but we need to use technology mindfully. Striking a balance between digital and in-person communication is essential to maintain meaningful relationships. So, while technology has greatly enhanced convenience, it's important not to lose the human touch.","annotations":["Balanced discussion of positive and negative impacts.","Personal example adds authenticity and supports the argument.","Clear conclusion that summarises the speaker's stance."]},{"id":"gm-87","type":"speaking","band":"8.0","title":"Speaking Part 1: Hometown and Daily Routine","prompt":"Where is your hometown? What do you like most about it? What is your daily routine like?","essay":"My hometown is a small coastal city called Brighton, located in the south of England. It's famous for its pebble beach and vibrant arts scene. What I like most about it is the relaxed atmosphere and the friendly community. Even though it's a city, it feels like a town because everything is within walking distance, and you often bump into people you know. The sea air is also incredibly refreshing, and I love taking walks along the promenade.\n\nAs for my daily routine, I'm an early riser. I usually wake up around 6:30 a.m., have a quick breakfast, and then go for a jog along the seafront. That's my favourite part of the day because it's quiet and peaceful. After that, I start work at 9 a.m. I work from home as a graphic designer, so I spend most of my day at my computer. I take a lunch break around noon, often meeting a friend for coffee. In the evening, I cook dinner, maybe watch a bit of TV, and read a book before bed. I try to be in bed by 11 p.m. to get enough rest. My routine is pretty consistent, which I find helps me stay productive and balanced.","annotations":["Natural, conversational tone appropriate for Part 1.","Provides detailed descriptions and personal preferences.","Uses a range of vocabulary and linking words smoothly."]},{"id":"gm-88","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Writing Task 2: University Education and Employment","prompt":"Some people think that universities should provide graduates with the knowledge and skills needed in the workplace. Others think that the true function of a university should be to give access to knowledge for its own sake. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","essay":"The role of universities has long been debated: should they focus on employability or on knowledge for its own sake? While both perspectives have merit, I believe that universities should balance both objectives to serve students and society effectively.\n\nThose who argue for workplace-oriented education emphasise that graduates need practical skills to secure employment. In today's competitive job market, employers often seek candidates with specific technical abilities and soft skills. Universities that offer internships, industry projects, and career-focused courses can better prepare students for the transition to work. For instance, engineering programmes that include cooperative placements produce job-ready graduates. This approach also benefits the economy by reducing skills gaps.\n\nOn the other hand, proponents of liberal education argue that universities should cultivate critical thinking, curiosity, and a broad intellectual foundation. Knowledge pursued for its own sake enriches individuals and society, fostering innovation and cultural understanding. Subjects like philosophy, literature, and history may not have direct vocational links but develop transferable skills such as analytical reasoning and communication. Moreover, a purely utilitarian education risks producing narrow specialists ill-equipped to adapt to a changing world.\n\nIn my view, the ideal university education integrates both. Students should acquire employable skills while also engaging with ideas that expand their horizons. Many universities already achieve this through elective courses and interdisciplinary programmes. Ultimately, a balanced approach ensures graduates are both competent professionals and thoughtful citizens.\n\nIn conclusion, while employability is important, universities must not abandon their broader mission. A synthesis of practical and theoretical learning best serves individuals and society.","annotations":["Discusses both views fully before offering a balanced opinion.","Uses examples and reasoning to support each perspective.","Clear structure with introduction, body paragraphs, and conclusion."]},{"id":"gm-89","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Writing Task 1: Pie Charts on Household Expenditure","prompt":"The pie charts below show the average household expenditure in a country in 1990 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"The pie charts compare the average household expenditure in a particular country in 1990 and 2020, broken down into five categories: housing, food, transportation, entertainment, and other.\n\nOverall, housing remained the largest expenditure category in both years, while entertainment consistently accounted for the smallest proportion. However, the proportion spent on food decreased significantly, whereas transportation and entertainment saw increases.\n\nIn 1990, housing accounted for 30% of total expenditure, followed by food at 25%, transportation at 15%, other at 20%, and entertainment at 10%. By 2020, housing had risen slightly to 32%, maintaining its top position. Food, however, dropped to 18%, a notable decline. Transportation increased to 20%, and entertainment rose to 15%. The 'other' category decreased to 15%.\n\nThe most striking change was the shift away from food, which fell by 7 percentage points, and the rise in transportation and entertainment, each increasing by 5 percentage points. This suggests that as incomes rose, households allocated more to discretionary items like travel and leisure. Meanwhile, the slight increase in housing indicates that accommodation costs continued to absorb a significant portion of budgets.\n\nIn summary, while housing remained the dominant expense, the period saw a reallocation of spending towards transportation and entertainment, reflecting changing lifestyles and priorities.","annotations":["Clear overview highlighting main similarities and differences.","Accurate data reporting with percentages and changes.","Good use of comparative language to describe trends."]},{"id":"gm-90","type":"speaking","band":"8.0","title":"Speaking Part 2: Describe a Memorable Journey","prompt":"Describe a journey you remember well. You should say: where you went, how you travelled, why you made the journey, and explain why it was memorable.","essay":"I'd like to talk about a trip I took to Japan a few years ago. It was a two-week journey, and I travelled by plane from London to Tokyo, then used the Shinkansen bullet train to visit several cities, including Kyoto and Osaka. The reason for the journey was partly to celebrate my graduation and partly to experience a completely different culture. I had always been fascinated by Japanese history and cuisine, so it was a dream trip.\n\nWhat made it memorable was the sheer contrast to my everyday life. The efficiency and cleanliness of the train system amazed me, and the politeness of the people was heartwarming. I remember standing at Shibuya Crossing, watching hundreds of people cross in perfect order—it was mesmerising. Another highlight was staying in a traditional ryokan in Kyoto, where I slept on a tatami mat and enjoyed a kaiseki dinner. I also visited Nara to see the friendly deer, which was a delightful experience.\n\nBut the most memorable moment was climbing Mount Fuji. It was challenging, especially the altitude, but reaching the summit at sunrise was breathtaking. The view from the top, with the sea of clouds below, was unforgettable. That journey taught me the value of stepping out of my comfort zone and embracing new experiences. It remains one of the best trips of my life.","annotations":["Rich descriptive detail that brings the journey to life.","Clear structure covering all bullet points from the prompt.","Expresses personal reflection on why it was memorable."]},{"id":"gm-91","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Writing Task 2: Reducing Private Car Use","prompt":"Some people believe that the best way to reduce traffic congestion in cities is to increase the cost of driving. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","essay":"Traffic congestion is a major problem in many urban areas, and some argue that raising the cost of driving is the most effective solution. While I agree that higher costs can help reduce car use, I believe that this measure alone is insufficient and must be combined with other strategies.\n\nIncreasing the expense of driving, such as through higher fuel taxes, congestion charges, or parking fees, can discourage people from using private cars. When driving becomes more expensive, individuals may opt for public transport, carpooling, or cycling, which can ease congestion. For example, London's congestion charge has reduced traffic in the city centre by a significant percentage. However, this approach disproportionately affects lower-income drivers who may have no alternative but to drive. Moreover, if public transport is inadequate, people will continue to drive despite the costs.\n\nTherefore, to effectively tackle congestion, higher driving costs should be part of a broader package. Governments must invest in reliable, affordable public transport, create safe cycling infrastructure, and promote flexible working arrangements. For instance, cities like Amsterdam have successfully reduced car dependency through excellent cycling networks and integrated public transport. Additionally, urban planning that places amenities within walking distance can reduce the need for travel.\n\nIn conclusion, while increasing the cost of driving can contribute to reducing congestion, it is not a standalone solution. A combination of pricing measures, improved public transport, and sustainable urban design is necessary to achieve lasting results.","annotations":["Clear position stated in introduction.","Balanced argument with examples.","Cohesive devices used effectively."]},{"id":"gm-92","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Writing Task 2: The Value of International Tourism","prompt":"International tourism has become a significant source of income for many countries. However, it can also cause problems. To what extent do the benefits outweigh the drawbacks?","essay":"International tourism is a double-edged sword, bringing economic gains but also potential social and environmental costs. In my view, the benefits generally outweigh the drawbacks, provided that tourism is managed sustainably.\n\nOn the positive side, tourism generates substantial revenue, creating jobs in hospitality, transport, and retail. For many developing countries, it is a vital source of foreign currency, funding infrastructure and public services. Moreover, cultural exchange fostered by tourism can promote mutual understanding and preserve local traditions. For instance, in countries like Thailand, tourism has helped revitalise traditional crafts and festivals.\n\nHowever, unregulated tourism can lead to environmental degradation, overcrowding, and cultural commodification. Popular destinations often suffer from pollution, resource depletion, and rising living costs for locals. The influx of tourists can also erode the authenticity of cultural experiences, turning heritage sites into commercial spectacles. These issues are evident in places like Venice, where mass tourism has driven residents away.\n\nNevertheless, these drawbacks can be mitigated through careful planning. Governments can implement visitor caps, promote eco-friendly tourism, and invest in infrastructure that benefits both tourists and residents. Sustainable tourism models, such as community-based tourism, ensure that profits remain local and cultural integrity is maintained.\n\nIn conclusion, while international tourism poses risks, its economic and cultural advantages are considerable. With proper management, the benefits can be maximised, and the negative impacts minimised.","annotations":["Sophisticated vocabulary and collocations.","Well-developed arguments with specific examples.","Effective counter-argument and solution."]},{"id":"gm-93","type":"writing","band":"7.5","title":"Writing Task 1: Bar Chart on Household Energy Use","prompt":"The bar chart below shows the average household energy consumption in four countries (A, B, C, and D) in 2020, measured in kilowatt-hours (kWh). Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"The bar chart illustrates the average household energy consumption in four countries—A, B, C, and D—in 2020, measured in kilowatt-hours (kWh). Overall, Country A had the highest consumption, while Country D had the lowest, with significant variations across the four nations.\n\nCountry A recorded the highest energy use, at approximately 12,000 kWh per household. This was more than double the consumption in Country B, which stood at around 5,500 kWh. Country C followed closely with 5,000 kWh, showing a relatively small difference of 500 kWh compared to Country B. In contrast, Country D consumed the least energy, at just 2,000 kWh per household.\n\nWhen comparing the countries, it is evident that energy consumption in Country A was substantially higher than in the other three. The gap between Country A and Country B was particularly pronounced, exceeding 6,000 kWh. Meanwhile, Countries B and C displayed similar levels of consumption, suggesting comparable energy use patterns. Country D, however, used less than half the energy of Country B and only about one-sixth of that of Country A.\n\nIn summary, the chart reveals marked disparities in household energy consumption among the four countries, with Country A being the largest consumer and Country D the smallest.","annotations":["Clear overview and main trends.","Accurate data comparison.","Logical paragraphing and linking."]},{"id":"gm-94","type":"speaking","band":"8.0","title":"Speaking Part 2: Describe a Skill You Learned","prompt":"Describe a skill you learned that you find useful. You should say: what the skill is, when and how you learned it, how you use it, and explain why you find it useful.","essay":"I'd like to talk about learning to code, which I picked up about three years ago during my first year at university. I started with Python through online tutorials and then took a formal course on web development. It was challenging at first, especially debugging, but I persevered by practising daily and working on small projects.\n\nNow, I use coding in various aspects of my life. For my studies, I've built data analysis scripts to handle research data efficiently. In my free time, I've created a personal website and even a simple app to track my fitness goals. I also occasionally freelance, which gives me some extra income.\n\nI find this skill incredibly useful for several reasons. Firstly, it's highly practical—technology is everywhere, and coding allows me to automate tasks, solve problems, and understand how digital tools work. Secondly, it's a creative outlet; I can bring ideas to life, like designing a website for a friend's small business. Thirdly, it's a valuable asset in the job market, opening doors to many career opportunities. Moreover, coding has improved my logical thinking and patience, as it requires breaking down complex problems into manageable steps. Overall, learning to code has been one of the most rewarding investments of my time, and I continue to learn new languages and frameworks to stay updated.","annotations":["Fluent and coherent with natural fillers.","Rich vocabulary and varied grammar.","Covers all bullet points fully."]},{"id":"gm-95","type":"speaking","band":"7.5","title":"Speaking Part 3: The Impact of Technology on Communication","prompt":"How has technology changed the way people communicate? Do you think these changes are positive or negative?","essay":"Technology has revolutionised communication in numerous ways. In the past, people relied on letters or face-to-face conversations, but now we have instant messaging, video calls, and social media. This has made communication much faster and more convenient. For example, I can video chat with my family overseas at any time, which was unimaginable a few decades ago. However, there are downsides. People sometimes prefer texting over meeting in person, which can lead to weaker interpersonal skills and feelings of isolation. Also, the constant connectivity can be stressful, as we feel pressured to respond immediately.\n\nOverall, I believe the changes are largely positive, but with caveats. The ability to connect globally has fostered understanding and collaboration. Businesses can operate across borders seamlessly, and individuals can maintain long-distance relationships. Yet, we must be mindful of over-reliance on digital communication. It's important to balance online interactions with face-to-face meetings to preserve the depth of human connection. In my opinion, technology is a tool, and its impact depends on how we use it. If used wisely, it enhances communication; if abused, it can hinder genuine interaction. So, while there are negatives, the benefits outweigh them when we use technology responsibly.","annotations":["Addresses both parts of the question.","Provides examples and balanced view.","Coherent and well-structured response."]},{"id":"gm-96","type":"writing","band":"8.0","title":"Writing Task 1: Line Graph on Internet Users","prompt":"The line graph below shows the percentage of the population using the internet in three countries (X, Y, and Z) from 2000 to 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","essay":"The line graph depicts the proportion of internet users in three countries—X, Y, and Z—over a 20-year period from 2000 to 2020. Overall, all three countries experienced a significant upward trend in internet usage, with Country X consistently having the highest percentage and Country Z the lowest.\n\nIn 2000, Country X had the highest internet penetration at around 40%, followed by Country Y at 20%, and Country Z at a mere 5%. Over the next decade, Country X's usage rose steadily, reaching 70% by 2010, while Country Y grew more rapidly, climbing to 50%. Country Z, despite starting from a low base, saw a gradual increase to 20% by 2010.\n\nFrom 2010 to 2020, the growth continued but at different rates. Country X's growth slowed, reaching 90% by 2020, indicating near-universal access. Country Y accelerated, surpassing 80% by 2020. Country Z experienced the most dramatic relative growth, albeit from a low starting point, ending at 60% in 2020. Notably, the gap between Country X and Country Z narrowed from 35 percentage points in 2000 to 30 in 2020, while Country Y nearly caught up with Country X.\n\nIn summary, internet usage expanded substantially in all three countries, with Country Z showing the fastest relative growth, though Country X remained the leader throughout the period.","annotations":["Clear overview and trend description.","Precise data references and comparisons.","Varied sentence structures and vocabulary."]},{"id":"gm-97","type":"speaking","band":"7.5","title":"Speaking Part 2: Describe a Memorable Journey","prompt":"Describe a memorable journey you have taken. You should say: where you went, how you travelled, what you did, and explain why it was memorable.","essay":"I'd like to describe a memorable journey I took to Japan a couple of years ago. I travelled with my best friend, and we spent two weeks exploring Tokyo, Kyoto, and Osaka. We flew from our home country to Tokyo, and then used the famous bullet trains to move between cities, which was an experience in itself.\n\nDuring the trip, we immersed ourselves in the culture. In Tokyo, we visited ancient temples like Senso-ji and also experienced the bustling Shibuya Crossing. In Kyoto, we wandered through the iconic Arashiyama Bamboo Grove and participated in a traditional tea ceremony. We also tried various local dishes, from sushi to ramen, and even stayed in a ryokan, a traditional Japanese inn, which was incredibly relaxing.\n\nThe journey was memorable for several reasons. Firstly, it was my first time travelling to a country with such a distinct culture, and the contrast with my own was eye-opening. Secondly, the people were exceptionally polite and helpful, which made navigating the language barrier easier. Thirdly, the scenery was breathtaking, especially the cherry blossoms in full bloom. But most importantly, the trip strengthened my friendship with my companion, as we shared countless new experiences and challenges. It was a perfect blend of adventure, relaxation, and cultural discovery, and I would love to go back someday.","annotations":["Engaging narrative with descriptive details.","Covers all prompt points clearly.","Good use of tenses and linking words."]}];